// ============================================================
// QUIZ QUESTION BANK — 210 questions across 9 categories
// cats: sql, nosql, perf, k8s, replication, transactions, vector, cloud, security
// ============================================================
// ============================================================
const allQuizQuestions = [
  // SQL & Relational
  {q:'Which PostgreSQL isolation level prevents ALL anomalies including phantom reads and write skew?',opts:['Read Committed','Repeatable Read','Serializable','Read Uncommitted'],ans:2,cat:'sql',exp:'Serializable is the strictest isolation level. It prevents dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantom reads, AND write skew. PostgreSQL implements it using Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI). CockroachDB uses serializable by default.'},
  {q:'What does MVCC stand for and what problem does it solve?',opts:['Multi-Value Concurrency Control — handles multiple values per key','Multi-Version Concurrency Control — allows reads without blocking writes','Multiple Version Cache Control — speeds up repeated queries','Managed Version Consistency Check — validates data integrity'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) keeps multiple versions of rows simultaneously. Readers see a consistent snapshot of the data without blocking or being blocked by writers. This is how PostgreSQL and MySQL InnoDB achieve high concurrency.'},
  {q:'In PostgreSQL, what does VACUUM do?',opts:['Backs up the database to disk','Removes dead row versions left by MVCC and reclaims storage','Optimizes index structures','Compresses table data for faster reads'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'VACUUM reclaims storage occupied by dead tuples — old row versions left behind by UPDATE and DELETE operations in MVCC. Without regular vacuuming, tables grow bloated. autovacuum runs automatically. VACUUM ANALYZE also updates query planner statistics.'},
  {q:'What is the difference between HAVING and WHERE?',opts:['WHERE filters before grouping; HAVING filters after grouping','HAVING is faster than WHERE','WHERE only works with JOINs; HAVING works with subqueries','There is no difference — they are interchangeable'],ans:0,cat:'sql',exp:'WHERE filters rows BEFORE GROUP BY runs — it can use indexes and is very efficient. HAVING filters AFTER GROUP BY — it operates on aggregated results. Rule: use WHERE for row-level filters, HAVING only for conditions on aggregates like COUNT() or SUM().'},
  {q:'Which index type in PostgreSQL is best for querying JSONB fields?',opts:['B-tree','Hash','GIN','BRIN'],ans:2,cat:'sql',exp:'GIN (Generalized Inverted Index) is designed for composite types like JSONB, arrays, and full-text search. It creates an index entry for each key/value in a JSONB document, enabling fast containment (@>) and existence (?) queries. B-tree indexes cannot index inside JSONB.'},
  {q:'What does CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY do differently from CREATE INDEX?',opts:['It creates the index faster using parallel workers','It creates the index without locking the table for writes','It creates a partial index','It creates a temporary index that is auto-dropped'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY builds the index without taking a write lock on the table — other DML operations can continue during the build. It takes longer (multiple passes over the table) but is safe for production. Plain CREATE INDEX takes an exclusive lock, blocking all writes.'},
  {q:'In a composite index on (user_id, created_at, status), which query can use this index?',opts:['WHERE created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY','WHERE status = \'active\'','WHERE user_id = 1 AND created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY','WHERE status = \'active\' AND created_at > NOW()'],ans:2,cat:'sql',exp:'Composite indexes work from left to right. A query must include the leftmost column(s) to use the index. WHERE user_id = 1 AND created_at > ... uses the first two columns. Queries starting with created_at or status alone cannot use this index effectively.'},
  {q:'What is the N+1 query problem?',opts:['Using LIMIT without ORDER BY returns N+1 rows unexpectedly','Loading N records then executing 1 additional query per record instead of joining','Having N+1 indexes on a table causing slow writes','A connection pool running out of connections under N users'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'N+1 occurs when an application fetches a list of N records, then executes 1 separate query for each to load related data — resulting in N+1 total queries. Fix: use JOINs, IN clauses, or ORM eager loading to load all data in 1-2 queries.'},

  // NoSQL
  {q:'What is the CAP theorem?',opts:['A distributed system can achieve Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance simultaneously','A distributed system can only guarantee 2 of: Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance','All distributed databases must choose between ACID and BASE','Cassandra, AWS, and PostgreSQL are the three major database paradigms'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'The CAP theorem (Brewer, 2000) states that a distributed system can only guarantee 2 of 3 properties during a network partition. CP systems (HBase, etcd) sacrifice availability. AP systems (Cassandra, DynamoDB) sacrifice consistency. CA is only possible without partitions (single-node PostgreSQL).'},
  {q:'In MongoDB, what is the difference between embedded documents and references?',opts:['Embedded documents are faster to insert; references are faster to query','Embedded: store related data inside the document (read together). References: link to another collection (updated independently)','References are MongoDB\'s equivalent of SQL JOINs and always preferred','Embedded documents are deprecated in MongoDB 7.0'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Embed data that is always read together (e.g., order items in an order document). Use references when data is updated independently or shared across documents. Embedding reduces reads but increases document size. References require $lookup (aggregation JOIN) to read together.'},
  {q:'What is a shard key in a distributed database?',opts:['The encryption key used to secure shard data','The field(s) used to determine which shard stores a row/document','A special index that spans multiple shards','The primary key of the first table created in a sharded database'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'The shard key determines data distribution across shards. Choosing a good shard key is critical: it must have high cardinality, uniform distribution, and align with query patterns. A bad shard key (like a boolean field) causes hotspots where one shard handles all writes.'},
  {q:'What consistency level in Cassandra provides the best balance of consistency and availability?',opts:['ONE','ALL','QUORUM','LOCAL_ONE'],ans:2,cat:'nosql',exp:'QUORUM requires a majority (RF/2 + 1) of nodes to respond. For RF=3, that\'s 2 nodes. This tolerates 1 node failure while maintaining consistency. LOCAL_QUORUM does the same but only within the local data center — ideal for multi-DC deployments to avoid cross-DC latency.'},
  {q:'What is eventual consistency?',opts:['Data is eventually deleted if not accessed','All replicas will converge to the same value given no new updates','Consistency checks run eventually in the background','Database transactions are eventually committed'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Eventual consistency means replicas may temporarily serve stale data after a write, but will converge to the same value if no new updates occur. Used by Cassandra (ONE consistency), DynamoDB (eventually consistent reads), and DNS. Faster than strong consistency but requires applications to tolerate stale reads.'},
  {q:'In Redis, which data structure would you use for a real-time leaderboard?',opts:['String — with INCR for scores','Hash — with user IDs as fields','Sorted Set — with score as the sort value','List — maintaining insertion order'],ans:2,cat:'nosql',exp:'Sorted Sets (ZADD, ZREVRANGE, ZREVRANK, ZINCRBY) are perfect for leaderboards. Members are sorted by their score with O(log N) add/update. ZREVRANGE gets the top-N players with scores. ZREVRANK gets a player\'s rank. ZINCRBY atomically updates scores.'},
  {q:'What does DynamoDB\'s Single Table Design achieve?',opts:['Stores only one type of entity per table for simplicity','Stores all entity types in one table using PK/SK prefixes to enable all access patterns efficiently','Limits tables to 1GB for performance','Ensures only one write happens at a time per table'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Single Table Design stores all entity types (users, orders, products) in one DynamoDB table, using partition key (PK) and sort key (SK) prefixes to separate them (USER#u1, ORDER#o1). This enables efficient access patterns like "get user + all their orders" in a single query.'},

  // Performance
  {q:'What does EXPLAIN ANALYZE do differently from EXPLAIN?',opts:['EXPLAIN ANALYZE checks for syntax errors before running','EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually executes the query and shows real row counts and timings','EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows the index creation command','EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs the query in a sandbox without modifying data'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'EXPLAIN shows the planner\'s estimated query plan without executing the query. EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually runs the query and shows real timings, actual row counts vs estimated, and buffer usage (with BUFFERS option). Discrepancies between estimated and actual rows indicate stale statistics — run ANALYZE.'},
  {q:'A PostgreSQL table has a B-tree index on (status, created_at). Which query CANNOT use this index efficiently?',opts:['WHERE status = \'active\' AND created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY','WHERE status = \'active\'','WHERE created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY','WHERE status IN (\'active\', \'pending\')'],ans:2,cat:'perf',exp:'Composite B-tree indexes work left-to-right. You must include the leftmost column (status) for the index to be used. WHERE created_at > ... alone skips the first column and forces a sequential scan or separate index. Solution: create a separate index on just created_at.'},
  {q:'What is connection pooling and why is it needed?',opts:['Grouping multiple queries into a single network packet','Reusing existing database connections to avoid the overhead of creating new ones','Caching query results for repeated identical queries','Distributing connections across multiple database replicas'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Opening a PostgreSQL connection takes ~50ms and ~5MB RAM. Web applications create hundreds of requests/sec — without pooling, this creates hundreds of connections constantly. PgBouncer maintains a pool of connections to PostgreSQL and multiplexes application requests, dramatically reducing overhead.'},
  {q:'What does "cache hit ratio" measure in a database?',opts:['Percentage of queries that used an index vs a sequential scan','Percentage of queries returned from query result cache','Percentage of data reads served from memory vs disk','Percentage of connections accepted vs rejected'],ans:2,cat:'perf',exp:'Cache hit ratio measures how often data is found in the buffer pool (memory) vs requiring a disk read. Target > 99%. Below 95% means your buffer pool (shared_buffers / innodb_buffer_pool_size) is too small for your working set. Each disk read is 100-1000x slower than a memory read.'},
  {q:'Which PostgreSQL feature would you use to track and analyze slow queries in production?',opts:['pg_stat_bgwriter','pg_stat_statements','pg_hba.conf','autovacuum'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'pg_stat_statements extension tracks all executed queries with their execution count, total time, mean time, rows affected, and more. Enable with CREATE EXTENSION pg_stat_statements. Then query it to find your slowest queries by mean execution time. Essential for production query optimization.'},

  // K8s & Operators
  {q:'Why must databases use StatefulSets rather than Deployments in Kubernetes?',opts:['StatefulSets are faster to deploy','StatefulSets provide stable network identity and persistent storage that survives pod restarts','Deployments don\'t support multiple replicas','StatefulSets have higher resource limits'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Deployments create pods with random names and no storage persistence — pods can be replaced anywhere. StatefulSets provide: stable pod names (pod-0, pod-1), stable DNS hostnames, and PVCs that follow pods across restarts. Databases require stable identity for replication and stable storage for data.'},
  {q:'What is the CloudNativePG operator used for?',opts:['Running ClickHouse on Kubernetes','Running PostgreSQL on Kubernetes with automatic HA, failover, and WAL backup','Monitoring all databases in a Kubernetes cluster','Managing Kubernetes secrets for database credentials'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'CloudNativePG (CNPG) is the CNCF-listed Kubernetes operator for PostgreSQL. It manages the full PostgreSQL lifecycle: cluster creation, primary/replica topology, automatic failover, WAL archiving to S3/GCS/Azure, connection pooling via PgBouncer sidecar, rolling updates, and Prometheus monitoring.'},
  {q:'What does a Kubernetes operator do?',opts:['Provides an admin UI for managing Kubernetes clusters','Automates application lifecycle using custom controllers and CRDs','Manages Kubernetes node security and access control','Schedules pods to appropriate nodes based on resource requirements'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'A Kubernetes operator extends the K8s API with CRDs (Custom Resource Definitions) and runs a controller that watches these resources. When you create a PostgreSQLCluster CRD, the operator\'s controller responds by creating StatefulSets, Services, ConfigMaps, and PVCs — encoding expert operational knowledge as code.'},
  {q:'In Kubernetes, what storage class setting is recommended for production databases?',opts:['Standard HDD storage for cost savings','NFS shared storage for easy data access','SSD-backed storage (gp3, pd-ssd, Premium SSD) with ReadWriteOnce access','Memory-backed tmpfs storage for maximum speed'],ans:2,cat:'k8s',exp:'Databases require low-latency, high-IOPS storage. Always use SSD-backed StorageClasses. ReadWriteOnce (RWO) means one pod mounts the volume at a time — correct for databases. NFS and shared storage have too much latency. tmpfs is not persistent. Never use default HDD storage for database PVCs.'},
  {q:'What is the purpose of PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) for databases?',opts:['Limits CPU and memory usage of database pods','Ensures at least N database pods remain running during voluntary disruptions (node drains)','Automatically scales database pods based on query load','Prevents unauthorized access to database pods'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'A PodDisruptionBudget defines how many pods can be unavailable during voluntary disruptions (node drain, cluster upgrade). For a 3-node database cluster, PDB maxUnavailable=1 ensures at least 2 nodes stay running. Without PDB, a node drain could take down all database pods simultaneously.'},

  // Replication & HA
  {q:'What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous replication?',opts:['Synchronous is faster; asynchronous is more reliable','Synchronous: primary waits for replica to confirm write before returning success. Asynchronous: primary returns success immediately, replica catches up later','Synchronous replication uses WAL; asynchronous uses triggers','Asynchronous replication is only available in enterprise database versions'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Synchronous replication: zero data loss (RPO=0) but higher write latency (waiting for replica ACK). Asynchronous: lower write latency but risk of data loss if primary fails before replica catches up. Most production setups use async with at least one sync standby for the failover target.'},
  {q:'What is replication lag?',opts:['The delay before replication is enabled after setup','The difference between the primary\'s current state and the replica\'s state','The time taken to configure a new replica','The maximum allowed transaction size on a replica'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Replication lag is how far behind a replica is from the primary. Measured as bytes (LSN difference in PostgreSQL) or seconds (time since last replicated transaction). High lag means reads from the replica are stale. Causes: slow network, slow replica disk, large transactions, replica CPU/IO saturation.'},
  {q:'What is split-brain in database replication?',opts:['When a database query plan splits into two execution paths','When two nodes simultaneously believe they are the primary and accept writes','When a table is split across two shards with different data','When a backup splits into incremental and full portions'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Split-brain occurs when a network partition causes two nodes to both believe they\'re the primary and accept writes. This results in diverged data that\'s difficult to reconcile. Prevented by quorum-based leader election (requires majority to elect a primary) and STONITH (fencing the old primary).'},
  {q:'What does Patroni do for PostgreSQL?',opts:['Provides a GUI for managing PostgreSQL databases','Automates PostgreSQL HA with automatic failover using a distributed configuration store','Adds horizontal sharding to PostgreSQL','Manages PostgreSQL query optimization'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Patroni is a PostgreSQL HA solution that uses a distributed configuration store (etcd, Consul, or ZooKeeper) to coordinate a cluster. It monitors primary health, handles automatic failover by promoting the best replica, fences the failed primary, and updates DNS/load balancer endpoints.'},
  {q:'What is PITR in database operations?',opts:['Point-in-Time Replication — copying data between time zones','Point-in-Time Recovery — restoring a database to a specific moment by replaying WAL from a backup','Parallel Insert Transaction Recording — a technique for fast bulk loads','Primary Instance Takeover and Restart — the failover process'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'PITR (Point-in-Time Recovery) restores a database to any specific moment in time by: 1) restoring a base backup, 2) replaying WAL/binlog up to the target timestamp. Essential for recovering from accidental data deletion or corruption. Requires continuous WAL archiving to S3/GCS.'},
  {q:'What does the Galera Cluster provide for MySQL/MariaDB?',opts:['Read scaling through async replicas','Synchronous multi-master replication — every node accepts writes, all committed on all nodes simultaneously','Automatic sharding across multiple MySQL instances','A GUI tool for MySQL cluster management'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Galera Cluster provides synchronous multi-master replication using wsrep API. Every write is committed on ALL nodes before returning success — zero replication lag between nodes. Any node can accept writes. Provides high availability and no data loss. Used by MariaDB Galera Cluster and Percona XtraDB Cluster.'},

  // ===== TRANSACTIONS & ISOLATION (25) =====
  {q:'What is a "dirty read"?',opts:['Reading a row that has a NULL value','Reading uncommitted changes made by another transaction','Reading data from a corrupted disk page','Reading stale data from a replica'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'A dirty read occurs when transaction A reads data that transaction B has modified but not yet committed. If B rolls back, A has acted on data that never existed. Only Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads — and PostgreSQL never allows them at any level.'},
  {q:'What is a non-repeatable read?',opts:['A query that cannot be executed twice due to locks','Reading the same row twice in one transaction and getting different values because another transaction committed an UPDATE in between','A read that fails and must be retried','Reading from a replica that has replication lag'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'A non-repeatable read: T1 reads a row, T2 updates and commits that row, T1 reads it again and sees a different value — within the same transaction. Prevented by Repeatable Read and Serializable levels, allowed under Read Committed.'},
  {q:'What is a phantom read?',opts:['Reading a row that was deleted but still appears in the index','A repeated range query returning NEW rows inserted by another committed transaction','Reading data through a corrupted B-tree pointer','A read served entirely from the OS page cache'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'A phantom read: T1 runs SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE age > 30, T2 inserts a matching row and commits, T1 re-runs the query and sees a new "phantom" row. It differs from a non-repeatable read because the row set changes, not an existing row\'s values. Prevented at Serializable (and in PostgreSQL, also at Repeatable Read via snapshots).'},
  {q:'What is write skew?',opts:['Uneven write distribution across shards','Two transactions read overlapping data, make disjoint writes based on what they read, and jointly violate a constraint neither violates alone','Writes that arrive out of order on a replica','A clock synchronization problem in distributed writes'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Classic example: hospital on-call — both doctors read "2 doctors on call", each removes themselves, both commit — now 0 on call, violating the "at least 1" rule. Snapshot isolation (Repeatable Read) allows this because the writes touch different rows. Only Serializable prevents write skew.'},
  {q:'What is a lost update?',opts:['An update that was written to WAL but not to the data files','Two transactions read the same value, both compute a new value, and the second commit silently overwrites the first','An update rolled back due to a constraint violation','An update lost during failover to a replica'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Lost update: T1 and T2 both read balance=100, T1 writes 110, T2 writes 120 — the +10 from T1 is lost. Prevented by SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (pessimistic locking), optimistic version columns, atomic updates (SET balance = balance + 10), or Repeatable Read/Serializable which abort the second writer.'},
  {q:'Which isolation level is the DEFAULT in PostgreSQL?',opts:['Read Uncommitted','Read Committed','Repeatable Read','Serializable'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'PostgreSQL defaults to Read Committed: each statement sees a snapshot taken at statement start, so it never sees uncommitted data but can see different data across statements in one transaction. MySQL InnoDB, by contrast, defaults to Repeatable Read.'},
  {q:'Which isolation level is the DEFAULT in MySQL InnoDB?',opts:['Read Committed','Repeatable Read','Serializable','Snapshot'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'InnoDB defaults to REPEATABLE READ, implemented with MVCC snapshots plus next-key locks (record + gap locks) that also block many phantom scenarios during locking reads. Many large MySQL shops switch to READ COMMITTED to reduce gap-lock contention and deadlocks.'},
  {q:'How does PostgreSQL implement SERIALIZABLE isolation?',opts:['By taking a full table lock for every transaction','Serializable Snapshot Isolation (SSI) — optimistic detection of dangerous read/write dependency patterns, aborting one transaction','Two-phase locking on every row read','By queueing all transactions and running them one at a time'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'PostgreSQL SSI runs transactions on snapshots (no read locks) while tracking read/write dependencies. When it detects a dangerous structure (two adjacent rw-antidependencies) that could produce a non-serializable outcome, it aborts one transaction with a serialization_failure (40001). Applications MUST retry on 40001.'},
  {q:'What does SELECT ... FOR UPDATE do?',opts:['Marks the rows as recently updated in statistics','Takes row-level exclusive locks on returned rows so other transactions block trying to lock or update them until commit','Creates a temporary copy of the rows for editing','Forces the query to bypass MVCC and read the latest data from disk'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'FOR UPDATE locks the selected rows (pessimistic locking). Other transactions attempting UPDATE/DELETE/FOR UPDATE on those rows wait. Use it to prevent lost updates in read-modify-write flows. Variants: FOR NO KEY UPDATE (weaker), FOR SHARE (read lock), and NOWAIT / SKIP LOCKED for queue patterns.'},
  {q:'What is the purpose of SKIP LOCKED?',opts:['Skip rows with corrupted lock metadata','Let a query ignore rows already locked by other transactions — the foundation of job-queue patterns in SQL','Skip lock acquisition for read-only queries','Bypass deadlock detection for faster commits'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED returns only rows not currently locked, instead of waiting. Multiple workers can each grab different pending jobs from the same table concurrently without blocking each other — the standard SQL work-queue pattern (used by Solid Queue, Oban, graphile-worker).'},
  {q:'What is a deadlock?',opts:['A transaction that holds a lock for more than 60 seconds','Two (or more) transactions each waiting for a lock the other holds — neither can ever proceed','A lock on a row that was deleted','When the lock table runs out of memory'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'T1 locks row A then wants row B; T2 locks row B then wants row A — circular wait. Databases detect this (PostgreSQL checks the waits-for graph after deadlock_timeout, default 1s) and abort one victim with a deadlock error. Mitigation: always acquire locks in a consistent order (e.g., by primary key).'},
  {q:'What does BEGIN; ... COMMIT; guarantee under ACID atomicity?',opts:['All statements run faster as a batch','Either every statement\'s effects are applied, or none are — partial results are never visible after a crash or rollback','Statements are executed in parallel','Other sessions cannot read any table used in the transaction'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Atomicity means the transaction is all-or-nothing. If the session dies or ROLLBACK runs mid-transaction, MVCC row versions created by it are never marked committed, so they are invisible and later vacuumed. WAL + commit records make this crash-safe.'},
  {q:'What is two-phase commit (2PC)?',opts:['Committing first to WAL and then to data files','A protocol for atomic commit across MULTIPLE nodes/databases: a coordinator asks all participants to PREPARE, and only if all vote yes does it send COMMIT','Running COMMIT twice for extra durability','Splitting a large commit into two smaller ones'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'2PC makes a distributed transaction atomic: phase 1 (prepare) — every participant durably promises it can commit; phase 2 — coordinator broadcasts commit or abort. Weakness: if the coordinator dies after prepare, participants block holding locks ("in-doubt" transactions). Spanner, CockroachDB, and XA transactions build on 2PC.'},
  {q:'What is optimistic concurrency control?',opts:['Assuming queries succeed and skipping error handling','Proceeding without locks, then validating at commit time (e.g., version-number check) and retrying on conflict','Always using the lowest isolation level for speed','Letting the application manage all locking'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Optimistic control assumes conflicts are rare: read a version/timestamp column, do your work, then UPDATE ... WHERE id=? AND version=?. If 0 rows update, someone else won — retry. Great for low-contention workloads; under high contention, pessimistic locks (FOR UPDATE) waste less work.'},
  {q:'In PostgreSQL Repeatable Read, when does the transaction take its snapshot?',opts:['At BEGIN','At the first query in the transaction','At every statement','At COMMIT'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'A common gotcha: BEGIN alone does not freeze the view. The snapshot is taken at the first non-transaction-control statement. Under Read Committed, a NEW snapshot is taken for every statement — that is the essential difference between the two levels.'},
  {q:'What error must applications handle when using SERIALIZABLE in PostgreSQL or CockroachDB?',opts:['Syntax error 42601','Serialization failure (SQLSTATE 40001) — the transaction must be retried from the start','Out of memory error','Unique violation 23505'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Serializable databases abort transactions whose interleaving cannot be serialized. This is not a bug — it is the contract: catch 40001 (and 40P01 deadlock), then rerun the ENTIRE transaction. CockroachDB clients and frameworks like Django/Rails need explicit retry loops.'},
  {q:'Which anomaly can still occur under snapshot isolation (PostgreSQL Repeatable Read)?',opts:['Dirty read','Non-repeatable read','Phantom read within the snapshot','Write skew'],ans:3,cat:'transactions',exp:'Snapshot isolation prevents dirty, non-repeatable, and phantom reads (each transaction sees one frozen snapshot). But write skew survives: two snapshots each look consistent, the writes touch different rows, and the combination violates an invariant. This is exactly why SERIALIZABLE exists.'},
  {q:'What is MySQL InnoDB\'s "gap lock"?',opts:['A lock on unused disk pages','A lock on the GAP between index records preventing other transactions from INSERTing into that range','A lock acquired during gap analysis of fragmented tables','The delay between lock request and grant'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Under Repeatable Read, InnoDB locking reads take next-key locks = record lock + gap lock. The gap lock blocks inserts into the index range, preventing phantoms — but it also causes surprising blocking and deadlocks. Read Committed disables gap locking (except for FK/duplicate checks).'},
  {q:'What does the ACID "D" (Durability) rely on in most databases?',opts:['RAID controllers only','Write-Ahead Logging — changes are fsynced to a sequential log before commit returns, so committed data survives a crash','Keeping all data in memory twice','Automatic cloud backups'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'On COMMIT, the database appends commit + change records to the WAL (redo log) and fsyncs it. Data pages can be written lazily later; after a crash, WAL replay reconstructs committed state. Settings like synchronous_commit=off or innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=2 trade durability for speed.'},
  {q:'What is a savepoint?',opts:['An automatic backup taken before every transaction','A named marker inside a transaction that you can ROLLBACK TO, undoing only the work after it','The point where WAL is flushed to disk','A snapshot used by replicas'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'SAVEPOINT sp1; ... ROLLBACK TO sp1; undoes statements after the savepoint while keeping the transaction alive. Essential for handling expected errors mid-transaction (e.g., trying an INSERT that may conflict) — in PostgreSQL, any error aborts the whole transaction unless you roll back to a savepoint.'},
  {q:'Long-running transactions are dangerous in PostgreSQL primarily because they...',opts:['use exponentially more CPU over time','hold back the xmin horizon so VACUUM cannot clean dead tuples, causing table and index bloat','automatically escalate to table locks after 5 minutes','disable the query planner\'s use of indexes'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'MVCC must keep every row version any open snapshot might still need. An idle-in-transaction session from hours ago pins the oldest visible xmin — VACUUM cannot remove newer dead tuples anywhere in the database. Result: bloat, slower scans, and wraparound risk. Set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout.'},
  {q:'Which statement about READ UNCOMMITTED in PostgreSQL is true?',opts:['It allows dirty reads for maximum speed','PostgreSQL accepts the syntax but silently treats it as READ COMMITTED — dirty reads are impossible','It is the default level','It disables WAL for the transaction'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'PostgreSQL\'s MVCC design has no mechanism to read uncommitted row versions from other transactions, so READ UNCOMMITTED simply behaves as READ COMMITTED. In SQL Server and MySQL, READ UNCOMMITTED genuinely permits dirty reads.'},
  {q:'What is the "read-modify-write" hazard and its simplest SQL-level fix?',opts:['Reading a value, computing in the app, writing back — fixed by doing the arithmetic atomically in SQL: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10','Reading from disk then memory — fixed by caching','Reading JSON then writing XML — fixed by using one format','Reading on a replica and writing on the primary — fixed by sticky sessions'],ans:0,cat:'transactions',exp:'SELECT balance → compute in app → UPDATE balance = <newvalue> loses concurrent updates. The atomic form UPDATE ... SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE id=1 lets the database serialize the operation via row locks — no lost updates, no explicit transaction juggling needed for the simple case.'},
  {q:'CockroachDB and Spanner default to which isolation level?',opts:['Read Committed','Snapshot','Serializable','Linearizable Read Uncommitted'],ans:2,cat:'transactions',exp:'Both default to SERIALIZABLE — the strongest SQL isolation — even as distributed databases. Spanner additionally offers external consistency (strict serializability) using TrueTime. CockroachDB added a READ COMMITTED option in v23.2 for workloads migrating from PostgreSQL that cannot tolerate retry loops.'},
  {q:'Under Read Committed, a report that runs 10 SELECTs in one transaction can be internally inconsistent because...',opts:['Read Committed disables indexes','each statement gets a NEW snapshot, so later queries can see commits that earlier queries did not','the WAL is flushed between statements','the planner caches stale statistics'],ans:1,cat:'transactions',exp:'Per-statement snapshots mean totals computed by query #1 may not match a breakdown computed by query #10 if other transactions committed in between. For multi-query consistent reporting, use BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ — one snapshot for the whole report.'},

  // ===== VECTOR & AI (25) =====
  {q:'What is a vector embedding?',opts:['A compressed binary copy of a document','A fixed-length array of floats produced by an ML model, where semantic similarity maps to geometric closeness','An index entry pointing to a document','A hash of the input text'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Embedding models (e.g., OpenAI text-embedding-3, BGE, E5) map text, images, or audio into a high-dimensional space (typically 384–3072 dimensions). "Dog" and "puppy" land near each other; "dog" and "spreadsheet" do not. Vector databases search this space for nearest neighbors.'},
  {q:'What does ANN stand for in vector search?',opts:['Artificial Neural Network','Approximate Nearest Neighbor — trading a little recall for orders-of-magnitude faster search','Automatic Node Numbering','Adaptive Numeric Normalization'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Exact nearest-neighbor search must compare the query to every vector — O(n) and hopeless at millions of vectors. ANN indexes (HNSW, IVF, DiskANN) return the closest neighbors with ~95-99% recall in milliseconds. Almost all production vector search is ANN.'},
  {q:'How does the HNSW index work?',opts:['It hashes vectors into fixed buckets','A multi-layer navigable small-world graph: search starts at a sparse top layer and descends through denser layers, greedily hopping toward the query','It sorts vectors along one dimension','It stores vectors in a B-tree keyed by magnitude'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) builds a layered proximity graph. Upper layers are express lanes with few long-range links; lower layers are dense. Search greedily walks toward the query vector layer by layer. Excellent recall/latency, but the graph lives in RAM — memory is the cost.'},
  {q:'What is the key difference between IVFFlat and HNSW in pgvector?',opts:['IVFFlat only works with integers','IVFFlat clusters vectors into lists and probes a few at query time (smaller, needs training, lower recall); HNSW is a graph (more RAM, slower builds, better recall/speed, no training step)','HNSW only supports cosine distance','IVFFlat is deprecated'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'IVFFlat k-means-clusters vectors into `lists`; queries scan only `probes` nearest clusters — build it AFTER loading data. HNSW builds a graph incrementally, needs no training, handles updates gracefully, and usually wins on recall-vs-latency — at the price of memory and slower index builds. HNSW is the default recommendation in pgvector.'},
  {q:'Which distance metric is most commonly used for text embeddings?',opts:['Hamming distance','Cosine similarity (or dot product on normalized vectors)','Manhattan distance','Levenshtein distance'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors, ignoring magnitude — appropriate because embedding magnitude rarely carries meaning. For unit-normalized vectors, cosine similarity and inner product give identical rankings, and many models (like OpenAI\'s) ship normalized. pgvector operators: <=> cosine, <#> inner product, <-> L2.'},
  {q:'What is RAG?',opts:['Random Access Grid — a storage layout','Retrieval-Augmented Generation — retrieve relevant documents via vector search and inject them into the LLM prompt as grounding context','Redundant Array of GPUs','Recursive Aggregation Graph'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'RAG pipelines: chunk documents → embed chunks → store in a vector database → at question time, embed the query, retrieve top-k similar chunks, and paste them into the LLM prompt. It grounds answers in your data without retraining a model, and it is the dominant use case driving vector database adoption.'},
  {q:'What is "chunking" in a RAG pipeline?',opts:['Compressing vectors to save space','Splitting documents into smaller passages before embedding, because embeddings represent limited spans of meaning and LLM context is finite','Batching insert operations','Partitioning the vector index across nodes'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Embedding a 100-page PDF as one vector destroys granularity. Chunking splits it into passages (commonly 200–1000 tokens, often with overlap) so retrieval returns precise, relevant sections. Chunk size/strategy (fixed, semantic, heading-based) is one of the highest-leverage RAG quality knobs.'},
  {q:'What is hybrid search?',opts:['Searching across two databases at once','Combining lexical/keyword scoring (BM25) with semantic vector similarity, fusing results (e.g., RRF) for better relevance','Using both SSD and RAM for the index','Mixing exact and approximate NN in one query'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Vectors capture meaning but can miss exact identifiers ("error E-4032", part numbers, names); BM25 nails exact terms but misses synonyms. Hybrid search runs both and merges rankings — Reciprocal Rank Fusion is the common technique. Weaviate, Qdrant, OpenSearch, and Elasticsearch support it natively.'},
  {q:'Why is metadata filtering (e.g., tenant_id = 42) non-trivial in vector databases?',opts:['Metadata cannot be stored with vectors','Post-filtering after ANN can return too few results, while naive pre-filtering breaks graph traversal — engines need filter-aware ANN search','Filters require a separate SQL database','It is trivial — all engines do it the same way'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'If you fetch top-10 then filter, all 10 may belong to other tenants — zero results despite matches existing. Filtering FIRST invalidates HNSW\'s precomputed graph paths. Qdrant builds filterable HNSW (payload indexes integrated into traversal); pgvector can combine B-tree + vector via iterative scans. Always test recall with your real filters.'},
  {q:'What is quantization in vector databases?',opts:['Limiting queries per second','Compressing vectors (e.g., float32 → int8 scalar, product quantization, or 1-bit binary) to cut memory 4–32x at some recall cost','Rounding similarity scores','Splitting vectors across shards'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'A million 1536-dim float32 vectors ≈ 6 GB of RAM before graph overhead. Scalar quantization (int8) cuts 4x; binary quantization 32x with a rescoring pass over original vectors to recover accuracy. Qdrant, Milvus, pgvector (halfvec/bit), and Elasticsearch all ship quantization — the #1 lever for vector cost.'},
  {q:'When is pgvector the RIGHT choice over a dedicated vector database?',opts:['Never — dedicated engines are always better','When you already run PostgreSQL, vectors count in the low millions to tens of millions, and you benefit from joining vectors with relational data under ACID','Only for toy demos under 1000 vectors','When you need multi-billion-vector scale'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'One database = one backup/HA/monitoring story, plus SQL joins between embeddings and business tables and transactional consistency (embedding row and source row commit together). Rule of thumb: pgvector to ~10-50M vectors on decent hardware; beyond that, or with heavy multi-tenant filtered search, dedicated engines (Qdrant/Milvus) earn their operational cost.'},
  {q:'What differentiates Milvus architecturally from Qdrant?',opts:['Milvus only supports images','Milvus is a disaggregated distributed system (separate query/data/index nodes over object storage) for billion-scale; Qdrant is a simpler single-binary/replicated design that excels at filtered search','Qdrant has no HNSW support','Milvus runs only on GPUs'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Milvus separates compute from storage (pulsar/kafka log + object store + independent node types) — operationally heavier but scales to billions of vectors and offers GPU indexes. Qdrant (Rust, single binary, Raft replication) is simpler to run and known for first-class payload filtering. Match the tool to your scale.'},
  {q:'What is Pinecone\'s main trade-off versus open-source vector databases?',opts:['It has the worst performance','Zero operations and elastic serverless scaling, but fully proprietary — no self-hosting, and cost/lock-in at scale','It supports only 128-dimension vectors','It cannot do metadata filtering'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Pinecone is the "just works" managed option — serverless indexes bill by reads/writes/storage with no cluster sizing. The trade: closed source, cloud-only, no exit to self-hosted, and at sustained high scale costs often exceed running Qdrant/Milvus yourself. Classic build-vs-buy.'},
  {q:'What role does Chroma typically play in the AI stack?',opts:['Billion-scale production serving','The embedded, developer-first vector store — runs in-process (SQLite + HNSW), default in LangChain/LlamaIndex tutorials, ideal for prototypes and small apps','A GPU cluster scheduler','An embedding model provider'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Chroma is to vector databases what SQLite is to SQL: pip install chromadb and you have persistent vector search inside your Python process — no server. Perfect for notebooks, local RAG assistants, and apps under a few million vectors; graduate to Qdrant/Milvus/pgvector for scale-out production.'},
  {q:'Elasticsearch and OpenSearch handle vector search via...',opts:['They cannot do vector search','k-NN using HNSW indexes (Lucene-based in ES, Lucene/NMSLIB/FAISS engines in OpenSearch), combinable with BM25 for hybrid relevance','Exporting to an external vector service','Storing vectors as keyword strings'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Both added dense_vector/knn_vector fields with HNSW under the hood. Their superpower is combining decades of text-search relevance (BM25, analyzers, filters, aggregations) with semantic scores in one query — compelling when you already run the ELK/OpenSearch stack for search or logs.'},
  {q:'What is "recall" in ANN search evaluation?',opts:['How fast the index rebuilds after a crash','The fraction of true nearest neighbors that the approximate search actually returned (e.g., recall@10 = 0.97)','The number of queries per second','How much memory the index recalls from disk'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Recall@k compares ANN results against exact brute-force ground truth. Tuning knobs trade recall for speed: HNSW ef_search / m, IVF probes, quantization rescoring. Production teams benchmark recall on THEIR data — a config giving 0.99 on random vectors may give 0.85 on clustered real embeddings.'},
  {q:'Why do vector databases store a "payload"/metadata alongside each vector?',opts:['To make backups larger','Because the vector alone is meaningless to applications — you need the source text/ID/attributes for display, filtering, and joining back to business data','To improve HNSW graph quality','Payloads are deprecated'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'A search returns vector IDs and distances — useless without the chunk text, document URL, tenant, timestamps, and permissions attached. Payload design also drives filtering performance: index the fields you filter on (Qdrant payload indexes, pgvector btree columns).'},
  {q:'What is the typical dimensionality of modern text embedding models?',opts:['2–8 dimensions','384–3072 dimensions (e.g., MiniLM 384, OpenAI text-embedding-3-small 1536, -large 3072)','Exactly 42','Over 1 million dimensions'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Small local models (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) output 384-dim; mid-size 768–1024 (BGE, E5, Cohere); OpenAI\'s text-embedding-3-large up to 3072 with Matryoshka truncation to trade quality for cost. Dimension × count × 4 bytes = your raw RAM bill, which is why quantization and MRL truncation matter.'},
  {q:'What does "semantic caching" for LLMs use a vector database for?',opts:['Storing model weights','Embedding incoming prompts and returning a cached response when a sufficiently similar prompt was answered before','Caching database query plans','Storing chat history as text'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Instead of exact-string caching (misses paraphrases), semantic caches embed each prompt and check for near-duplicates above a similarity threshold, serving the stored answer and skipping an expensive LLM call. Redis, Qdrant, and pgvector are common backends for this pattern.'},
  {q:'A RAG system returns irrelevant chunks. Which is usually the FIRST thing to investigate?',opts:['Switching cloud providers','Chunking strategy and embedding model choice — then retrieval settings (top-k, hybrid search, reranking)','Increasing the LLM temperature','Adding more GPUs'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Retrieval quality dominates RAG quality. Debug in order: are chunks semantically coherent (not split mid-thought)? Is the embedding model suited to your domain/language? Then tune top-k, add hybrid BM25, and add a cross-encoder reranker on the top-50. The LLM can only answer from what retrieval feeds it.'},
  {q:'What is a reranker in a retrieval pipeline?',opts:['A load balancer for vector nodes','A cross-encoder model that rescores the top-N retrieved candidates by jointly reading query + document, more accurate but slower than embedding similarity','A tool that rebuilds HNSW nightly','A component that sorts results by date'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Bi-encoders (embeddings) score query and document independently — fast but coarse. Cross-encoders (e.g., Cohere Rerank, bge-reranker) read the pair together and produce sharper relevance. Standard pattern: ANN retrieves top-50 cheaply, reranker reorders, top-5 go to the LLM.'},
  {q:'How does DiskANN differ from HNSW?',opts:['It is exact, not approximate','It is designed for SSD-resident indexes — graph on disk with compressed vectors in RAM — serving billion-vector datasets on modest memory','It only works on GPUs','It requires no index build'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'HNSW wants the whole graph in RAM — expensive at billions of vectors. DiskANN (Microsoft; used in Milvus, Azure AI Search, pgvectorscale\'s StreamingDiskANN) keeps quantized vectors in memory for navigation and full vectors/graph on NVMe, cutting cost dramatically with modest latency increase.'},
  {q:'What consistency behavior should you expect from most dedicated vector databases?',opts:['Full ACID transactions across collections','Eventual/tunable consistency — recently upserted vectors may not be immediately searchable; pgvector inside Postgres is the notable ACID exception','Serializable isolation by default','Two-phase commit with your primary database'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Most vector engines batch inserts into index segments; search visibility can lag writes (Milvus offers tunable consistency levels; Qdrant applies WAL then indexes). If your app requires read-your-writes between source data and embeddings, pgvector\'s transactional guarantee is a genuine architectural advantage.'},
  {q:'In pgvector, which operator finds cosine distance, and how do you index for it?',opts:['<-> with a hash index','<=> with an HNSW index created USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)','LIKE with a GIN index','<@> with BRIN'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'ORDER BY embedding <=> query LIMIT 10 does cosine-distance kNN. Create the matching index: CREATE INDEX ON items USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops); — the opclass MUST match the operator or the index is ignored. L2 uses <-> with vector_l2_ops; inner product <#> with vector_ip_ops.'},
  {q:'Your embeddings come from model A, but you query with vectors from model B. What happens?',opts:['The database converts them automatically','Garbage results — different models produce incompatible vector spaces; you must embed documents and queries with the SAME model (and version)','Results are identical if dimensions match','An error is always raised'],ans:1,cat:'vector',exp:'Even at identical dimensionality, two models place concepts at unrelated coordinates. Matching dimensions means no error is thrown — just silently terrible similarity scores. Model (and version) is part of your schema: re-embedding the whole corpus is required when you change it. Plan for that migration path.'},

  // ===== CLOUD & WAREHOUSE (20) =====
  {q:'What architectural idea made Snowflake famous?',opts:['Running entirely in browser WASM','Separating storage (object storage) from compute (independently sized virtual warehouses) so multiple workloads scale and bill independently','Storing all data in RAM','Using blockchain for consistency'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Data lives once in cloud object storage; any number of "virtual warehouses" (compute clusters) query it concurrently without contention — ETL, BI, and data science each get their own right-sized, per-second-billed compute. This decoupling is now the standard cloud warehouse design.'},
  {q:'How does BigQuery pricing fundamentally differ from a provisioned warehouse?',opts:['It is free for the first petabyte','On-demand queries bill per TB of data SCANNED (plus storage), so partitioning, clustering, and selecting fewer columns directly cut cost','It bills per row returned','Flat monthly fee regardless of usage'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'SELECT * on a 10 TB table costs ~10 TB of scan even with LIMIT 10. Column pruning (columnar storage), partition filters (_PARTITIONDATE), and clustering reduce scanned bytes — the core BigQuery cost skill. Slot reservations offer capacity-based pricing for predictable heavy usage.'},
  {q:'What is a "lakehouse"?',opts:['A warehouse with a scenic view','ACID table formats (Delta Lake, Iceberg, Hudi) layered over data-lake object storage, so one open copy of data serves both warehouse SQL and ML/streaming workloads','A lake of backup files','Any database hosted in the cloud'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Traditional pattern: lake for cheap raw storage + separate proprietary warehouse for BI = two copies, drift, and lock-in. Lakehouse formats add transactions, schema evolution, and time travel directly on Parquet files in S3/GCS/ADLS — query with Databricks, Trino, Spark, DuckDB, Snowflake, or BigQuery.'},
  {q:'What is Apache Iceberg?',opts:['A cooling system for datacenters','An open table format providing ACID transactions, hidden partitioning, schema evolution, and time travel over data lake files — now supported by virtually every engine','A Kubernetes operator for Spark','A compression codec'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Iceberg tracks table state via metadata/manifest files, enabling atomic commits, safe concurrent writers, partition evolution without rewrites, and snapshot time travel. Snowflake, BigQuery (BigLake), Redshift, Databricks, Trino, and DuckDB all read/write it — it has become the neutral interchange layer of the lakehouse era.'},
  {q:'Aurora differs from standard RDS PostgreSQL/MySQL primarily by...',opts:['supporting more SQL syntax','a redesigned distributed storage layer (6 copies across 3 AZs, log-structured) giving faster failover, up to 15 low-lag replicas, and better write throughput','being free','running on-premises'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Aurora sends only WAL/redo records to a purpose-built storage service that materializes pages — less network I/O, storage that auto-grows, replicas reading the SAME storage (typically <20ms lag), and ~30s failovers. RDS is managed EBS-backed community engines; Aurora is a cloud-native re-architecture that costs more.'},
  {q:'What is "scale to zero" in serverless databases like Neon?',opts:['Deleting the database when idle','Suspending compute entirely during inactivity (storage persists), so idle databases cost only storage — with a cold-start delay on the next query','Reducing replicas to zero but keeping the primary','Zero-downtime scaling'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Neon detaches compute from storage: after ~5 minutes idle, compute suspends; the next connection cold-starts it in roughly half a second. A fleet of dev/preview/staging databases costs near-nothing while idle — the economic unlock for database-per-branch workflows.'},
  {q:'What is database branching (Neon, PlanetScale)?',opts:['Storing the database in a Git repository','Copy-on-write clones of a database that are instant and cheap — a full isolated environment per pull request, developer, or test run','Splitting a table across regions','A backup naming convention'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Because storage is log-structured/copy-on-write, a branch initially shares all pages with its parent and only stores divergent changes — creating one takes seconds regardless of size. CI can spin a branch per PR with production-shaped data, run migrations and tests, then delete it.'},
  {q:'Supabase generates instant REST APIs from your schema using...',opts:['Manually written Express routes','PostgREST — every table/view becomes a REST endpoint, with PostgreSQL Row Level Security enforcing per-user authorization','GraphQL only','Stored procedures exported as SOAP'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'PostgREST introspects the schema and serves CRUD with filtering/embedding via HTTP. Auth issues JWTs whose claims RLS policies check (auth.uid() = user_id) — authorization lives in the database, so the same rules protect REST, GraphQL, and direct SQL. The Postgres-native BaaS pattern.'},
  {q:'What is Vitess (the engine under PlanetScale)?',opts:['A MySQL GUI client','A sharding middleware/orchestration layer that scaled MySQL at YouTube — transparent query routing across shards, resharding, and connection pooling','A storage engine replacing InnoDB','A backup tool'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Vitess (CNCF graduated) presents many sharded MySQL instances as one logical database: VTGate routes queries by keyspace/shard key, handles cross-shard scatter-gather, online resharding, and massive connection multiplexing. GitHub, Slack, and Shopify-scale MySQL runs on it.'},
  {q:'DynamoDB pricing and capacity revolve around...',opts:['CPU cores provisioned','Read/Write Capacity Units (or on-demand request billing) — cost follows request volume and item size, making access-pattern design the schema design','GB of RAM','Number of tables'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'1 RCU = one strongly consistent 4KB read/sec; 1 WCU = one 1KB write/sec. You model tables around known access patterns (single-table design) because queries outside your key design require expensive scans. On-demand mode bills per request — great for spiky traffic.'},
  {q:'What is a "zero-ETL" integration (e.g., Aurora → Redshift)?',opts:['Deleting your ETL team','Managed near-real-time replication from an OLTP database into the warehouse without building pipelines — transactional data becomes queryable for analytics in seconds','Running analytics directly on the OLTP primary','A marketing term with no implementation'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'AWS zero-ETL streams Aurora/RDS changes into Redshift automatically (similarly BigQuery with Datastream, Snowflake with Snowpipe Streaming). It removes the classic nightly-batch CDC pipeline for the common "analyze my operational data" case — though transformations still need dbt or similar downstream.'},
  {q:'Snowflake "Time Travel" lets you...',opts:['Schedule queries for the future','Query or restore tables AS OF a past point (default 1 day, up to 90 on Enterprise) — recovering from bad UPDATEs or drops with UNDROP','Slow down queries deliberately','Replay WAL on RDS'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'SELECT * FROM orders AT (TIMESTAMP => \'...\') or CREATE TABLE restored CLONE orders BEFORE (STATEMENT => \'<query-id>\'), plus UNDROP TABLE. Because storage is immutable micro-partitions, historical versions are cheap to retain — a lifesaver for fat-fingered DML.'},
  {q:'When does DuckDB beat a cloud warehouse for an analytics task?',opts:['Never','When data fits on one machine (up to ~100s of GB): querying local/S3 Parquet in-process with zero infra, zero network egress, and zero per-query billing','Only for datasets under 1 MB','When you need 1000 concurrent BI users'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'A laptop with DuckDB scans Parquet at GB/s — many "big data" jobs are actually medium data. Pipelines increasingly pre-process with DuckDB (dbt-duckdb), pushing only serving workloads to Snowflake/BigQuery. Warehouses win on huge scale, concurrency, governance, and sharing — not on a 50 GB ad-hoc analysis.'},
  {q:'What is Snowflake\'s zero-copy cloning?',opts:['Copying tables with rsync','CREATE TABLE dev CLONE prod — an instant, metadata-only copy that shares underlying storage until either side changes (copy-on-write)','A backup to a second region','Cloning virtual warehouses'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Clones point at the same immutable micro-partitions, so cloning a 50 TB database takes seconds and costs nothing until data diverges. Standard workflow for spinning up dev/test environments against production-shaped data — the same CoW idea Neon applies to Postgres branching.'},
  {q:'Redshift Spectrum / BigQuery external tables / Snowflake external tables all enable...',opts:['Faster inserts','Querying data IN PLACE on object storage (S3/GCS) with warehouse SQL, without loading it first — at reduced performance versus native storage','Cross-cloud replication','Automatic indexing of S3'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'External tables treat the lake as queryable: join hot native tables with cold Parquet history in S3, paying scan costs but no load/duplication. With Iceberg support maturing, the boundary between "external" and "native" tables is dissolving across all three warehouses.'},
  {q:'The most common cause of surprise Snowflake bills is...',opts:['Storage costs','Warehouses left running or oversized — compute bills per second while running, and auto-suspend/auto-resume plus right-sizing are the primary cost controls','Time Travel retention','Too many users'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Storage is cheap ($23/TB-month range); compute dominates. An X-Large warehouse (16 credits/hr) idling overnight burns real money. Set AUTO_SUSPEND=60s, size warehouses to workload, separate workloads so BI spikes don\'t force ETL cluster sizes, and monitor with resource monitors + query_history.'},
  {q:'What is Delta Lake\'s transaction log (_delta_log)?',opts:['A user audit log','An ordered JSON/parquet commit log on object storage that defines table state — enabling ACID, time travel, and concurrent writer coordination via optimistic concurrency','Kafka topic for CDC','The Spark driver log'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Every write commits a numbered JSON action file (add/remove files, metadata) atomically via object-store conditional puts; readers reconstruct the table from the log (with checkpoint parquet snapshots). Same core idea as Iceberg metadata trees — the log IS the table.'},
  {q:'Firestore vs Cloud SQL on GCP — the key decision factor is...',opts:['Price only','Data model and access pattern: Firestore for hierarchical documents, realtime sync, offline mobile, massive fan-out reads; Cloud SQL for relational integrity, joins, and complex ad-hoc queries','Firestore is newer so always better','Cloud SQL cannot scale'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'Firestore shines for mobile/web app state with realtime listeners and per-document security rules, but aggregations/joins are limited and query patterns must be pre-planned (composite indexes). Cloud SQL (or AlloyDB) gives full SQL. Many apps use both: Firestore for client sync, Postgres for the system of record.'},
  {q:'What does "separation of storage and compute" enable operationally?',opts:['Nothing — it is marketing','Independent scaling (grow storage without compute), instant compute resize/pause, multiple isolated compute clusters on one dataset, and fast recovery since data outlives compute','Lower network usage','Stronger isolation levels'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'When object storage holds the truth, compute becomes disposable: pause it (Neon scale-to-zero), resize it (Snowflake XS→3XL in seconds), or run several without copies (ETL + BI + ML). The pattern spans Snowflake, BigQuery, Aurora, Neon, Milvus, and modern Kafka redesigns alike.'},
  {q:'MotherDuck extends DuckDB with...',opts:['A mobile app','A managed cloud service featuring hybrid execution — queries can split between your local DuckDB and cloud compute, plus sharing and a serverless catalog','GPU acceleration','A rewrite in Java'],ans:1,cat:'cloud',exp:'MotherDuck (from DuckDB Labs partners) hosts DuckDB in the cloud with per-user "ducklings" (isolated compute), differential storage, and hybrid query plans that read local files and cloud tables in one SQL statement — a laptop-to-cloud analytics continuum rather than a monolithic warehouse.'},

  // ===== SECURITY & OPS (20) =====
  {q:'What is the single most important rule for database credentials in application code?',opts:['Rotate them yearly','Never hardcode them — inject via environment/secret managers (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, K8s Secrets) with rotation, and never commit them to Git','Share one superuser account for simplicity','Encode them in base64 in the repo'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Credentials in code leak through repos, images, and logs. Use a secrets manager with automatic rotation; in Kubernetes, mount Secrets (ideally synced from an external manager via External Secrets Operator) and prefer IAM-based auth (RDS IAM, Cloud SQL IAM) which eliminates long-lived passwords entirely.'},
  {q:'What is the principle of least privilege for database users?',opts:['All apps share the admin account','Each application/service gets a role with ONLY the permissions it needs (specific schemas, tables, operations) — never superuser, never blanket GRANT ALL','Users should request permissions per query','Only DBAs may run SELECT'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'An app that only reads orders should have SELECT on orders — nothing else. A compromised service then cannot drop tables, read user PII elsewhere, or escalate. Implement with roles: role per service, GRANT specific privileges, and separate migration users (DDL) from runtime users (DML).'},
  {q:'What is SQL injection and its primary defense?',opts:['Injecting SQL performance hints; defense is query plans','Attacker-controlled input concatenated into SQL strings executes as code; defense is parameterized queries/prepared statements — never string concatenation','A vaccine for databases','Malware in SQL Server; defense is antivirus'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'"...WHERE name = \'" + userInput + "\'" lets input like \' OR 1=1;-- rewrite your query. Parameterized queries ($1, ?) send SQL and data separately so input can never become code. ORMs do this by default — injection today usually enters via raw-SQL escape hatches and string-built ORDER BY clauses.'},
  {q:'What is PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS)?',opts:['Encrypting rows on disk','Per-row access policies enforced by the database itself (e.g., USING tenant_id = current_setting(\'app.tenant\')) — every query is automatically filtered regardless of application bugs','Row-level replication','Locking rows for updates'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON orders USING (tenant_id = current_tenant()); makes cross-tenant leakage impossible even if application WHERE clauses are forgotten. The backbone of Supabase authorization and the strongest multi-tenancy guard in shared-schema SaaS. Watch for: superuser/table-owner bypass and policy performance.'},
  {q:'Encryption "at rest" vs "in transit" — what covers what?',opts:['They are the same thing','At rest: data files/backups encrypted on disk (TDE, encrypted EBS/PD, LUKS). In transit: TLS on client connections and replication links. Production needs BOTH','At rest covers the network','In transit covers backups'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Disk encryption defeats stolen-volume/snapshot attacks; TLS (sslmode=verify-full, require_secure_transport) defeats network sniffing and MITM. Neither protects against a compromised app with valid credentials — that is what least privilege, RLS, and auditing address. Cloud providers encrypt at rest by default; enforcing TLS is on you.'},
  {q:'What is pg_hba.conf?',opts:['PostgreSQL\'s backup schedule','Host-Based Authentication config — rules mapping (connection type, database, user, client IP) to auth methods (scram-sha-256, cert, reject)','The HA failover configuration','Table partitioning rules'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'pg_hba.conf is PostgreSQL\'s network gate: e.g., hostssl all app 10.0.0.0/8 scram-sha-256. Classic incidents: "host all all 0.0.0.0/0 trust" (passwordless internet access) or md5 lingering instead of scram-sha-256. Order matters — first matching rule wins.'},
  {q:'Why should databases NEVER be directly exposed to the public internet?',opts:['Latency is too high','They are constantly scanned and brute-forced; breaches like exposed MongoDB/Elasticsearch instances leaked billions of records. Use private subnets, VPC peering, bastion/VPN, or at minimum strict IP allowlists','Public IPs cost extra','TLS does not work over the internet'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Shodan indexes open 5432/3306/27017/9200 ports within hours. Early MongoDB and Elasticsearch shipped with no auth by default — resulting in mass ransom incidents. Rule: databases live in private subnets; access flows through the application tier, a bastion, or managed proxies (RDS Proxy, Cloud SQL Auth Proxy).'},
  {q:'What is the 3-2-1 backup rule?',opts:['3 backups per day, 2 admins, 1 restore test','3 copies of data, on 2 different media/systems, with 1 offsite (and ideally offline/immutable) copy','3 replicas, 2 regions, 1 cloud','Restore in 3 hours, verify in 2, report in 1'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Replication is NOT backup — a DROP TABLE replicates instantly. 3-2-1 plus immutability (S3 Object Lock, separate account) also survives ransomware that targets backups. And the real rule zero: a backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup — schedule restore drills.'},
  {q:'What are RPO and RTO?',opts:['Read/Write performance metrics','RPO: maximum acceptable data LOSS (time since last recoverable point). RTO: maximum acceptable DOWNTIME to restore service. They drive backup frequency and HA architecture','Replication protocol options','PostgreSQL configuration parameters'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'RPO 5min ⇒ continuous WAL archiving/PITR, not nightly dumps. RTO 2min ⇒ hot standby with automated failover, not restore-from-S3 (hours). Every backup/HA design conversation should START with these two numbers — they determine cost and architecture.'},
  {q:'What is a PostgreSQL "logical dump" (pg_dump) vs a "physical backup" (pg_basebackup/WAL-G)?',opts:['They are identical','Logical: portable SQL/archive of schema+data, restore anywhere, but slow at scale and a single point in time. Physical: byte-level copy + WAL archive enabling PITR and fast large restores, but version/architecture-bound','Logical backs up logs; physical backs up tables','pg_dump is deprecated'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'pg_dump is perfect for small DBs, migrations, and per-table restores. Beyond tens of GB and for real RPO targets, physical base backups + continuous WAL archiving (pgBackRest, WAL-G) give point-in-time recovery to any second. Production Postgres runs both worlds: PITR primary, logical for portability.'},
  {q:'What is an audit log in database security?',opts:['The slow query log','A tamper-evident record of who did what and when (logins, DDL, sensitive reads/writes) — via pgAudit, MySQL audit plugins, or cloud-native audit — required by SOC 2, PCI-DSS, HIPAA','WAL by another name','Kubernetes events'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'When an incident happens, "which user read the customers table last Tuesday?" must be answerable. pgAudit classes (READ/WRITE/DDL/ROLE), server audit logs shipped to immutable storage (SIEM), and alerts on anomalies (mass exports, off-hours DDL) turn the database from a black box into evidence.'},
  {q:'Redis was historically exploited on open networks because...',opts:['TLS was mandatory','It shipped with NO authentication by default and commands like CONFIG SET dir allowed writing files (cron/SSH keys) — leading to mass compromises; modern practice: requirepass/ACLs, protected-mode, bind to private interfaces, rename dangerous commands','It only ran as root','Its port was 80'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Attackers used exposed Redis to write their SSH key via CONFIG SET dir /root/.ssh + SAVE. Redis 6+ added ACLs (per-user command/key permissions) and TLS; protected-mode refuses external connections without auth. Never expose 6379; treat Redis AUTH + ACL + network policy as mandatory.'},
  {q:'What is Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)?',opts:['TLS for databases','Engine-level encryption of data files, logs, and backups on disk, transparent to applications — native in SQL Server/Oracle/MySQL Enterprise; PostgreSQL typically relies on filesystem/volume encryption instead','Encrypting query text','Column hashing'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'TDE defeats offline attacks on stolen files/backups with zero app changes; it does NOT protect data from anyone with database credentials. Community PostgreSQL lacks built-in TDE — cloud disk encryption (default on RDS/Cloud SQL) or LUKS covers the same threat model. For field-level secrecy, use application-layer or pgcrypto encryption.'},
  {q:'A DBA leaves the company. What is the FIRST database-security action?',opts:['Send a farewell email','Disable/rotate their credentials and any shared secrets they had access to (superuser passwords, service accounts, SSH keys, secret-manager tokens) immediately as part of offboarding','Wait for quarterly review','Rename the database'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Offboarding runbooks must cover databases: personal accounts disabled, shared credentials rotated (this is why per-human accounts + SSO beat shared logins), bastion/VPN access revoked, and audit review of recent activity. IAM-integrated auth (Cloud SQL IAM, RDS IAM) makes this a single identity-provider action.'},
  {q:'What is connection exhaustion and its standard mitigation?',opts:['Running out of disk','Too many client connections (each Postgres backend ≈ a process with real memory) causing refused connections/OOM; mitigated with connection poolers (PgBouncer/RDS Proxy) and sane per-service limits','Network cable failure','Too many databases per server'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Serverless/lambda apps and microservice fleets can open thousands of connections; Postgres max_connections=100 default exists for a reason. PgBouncer in transaction mode multiplexes thousands of clients over tens of server connections. Also an availability-security issue: connection floods are an easy DoS without limits.'},
  {q:'Why separate the "migration" DB user from the "runtime" DB user?',opts:['Migrations run faster','Runtime app users need only DML (SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE); DDL rights (CREATE/ALTER/DROP) belong to a separate migration role used only in deploy pipelines — so a compromised app cannot alter schema or drop tables','Postgres requires it','To simplify passwords'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'This is least privilege applied to schema changes: the web app\'s credentials physically cannot DROP TABLE users even if fully compromised via injection or leaked secrets. CI/CD runs migrations with the privileged role, ideally with approval gates and audit logging.'},
  {q:'What does "immutable backups" protect against that ordinary backups may not?',opts:['Disk full errors','Ransomware and malicious insiders that encrypt or DELETE backups along with production — immutability (S3 Object Lock/WORM, separate account, offline copies) makes backups undeletable for the retention window','Replication lag','Schema drift'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Modern ransomware hunts backups first. If your backup bucket is writable/deletable with the same credentials as production, it is part of the blast radius. Object Lock compliance mode + cross-account replication + periodic offline copies ensure a recovery path even when the attacker has your keys.'},
  {q:'What is the safe pattern for giving analysts access to production data?',opts:['Read-only superuser on the primary','A read replica (or warehouse copy) with masked/limited PII, per-user accounts, query limits — never ad-hoc heavy queries on the OLTP primary','Shared CSV exports by email','Direct access with the app\'s credentials'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Analyst queries (giant scans, missing WHERE) can take down production. Route BI to replicas or the warehouse, apply column masking/views for PII (GDPR data minimization), give personal SSO-backed accounts for auditability, and set statement_timeout. The primary serves customers; analytics gets a copy.'},
  {q:'CVE hygiene for self-hosted databases means...',opts:['Reading CVEs annually','Tracking security releases (Postgres minor versions, MySQL CPUs, Redis advisories) and patching promptly — minor version upgrades are low-risk and contain the fixes; automate with operators/managed services','Disabling the network','Renaming binaries'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'PostgreSQL minor releases (16.3→16.4) are drop-in binary swaps carrying security fixes — running 3 years behind is unforced risk. Kubernetes operators (CNPG) roll minor updates safely; managed services patch for you within maintenance windows. Subscribe to pgsql-announce / vendor security lists.'},
  {q:'What is data masking / anonymization for non-production environments?',opts:['Compressing the data','Replacing real PII with realistic fake values (or redacting) when cloning prod → staging/dev, so lower-security environments never hold sensitive data — via postgresql_anonymizer, custom ETL, or platform features','Encrypting dev databases twice','Renaming columns'],ans:1,cat:'security',exp:'Dev/staging environments have weaker controls and broader access — copying raw production PII into them violates GDPR data minimization and is a common breach source. Mask on the way in (deterministic fakes keep joins working), and prefer synthetic seed data where possible. Neon/PlanetScale-style branch workflows should include masking steps for regulated data.'},

  // ===== SQL & RELATIONAL — ADDITIONS (22) =====
  {q:'What is the difference between a clustered and non-clustered index?',opts:['Clustered indexes only exist in clusters of servers','A clustered index determines the physical order of table rows (one per table — e.g., InnoDB primary key); non-clustered indexes are separate structures pointing to rows','Non-clustered indexes are always faster','Clustered indexes cannot be unique'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'In MySQL InnoDB, the table IS the clustered index (a B-tree ordered by primary key); secondary indexes store the PK as the row pointer — which is why long primary keys bloat every index. PostgreSQL has no clustered indexes (heap tables); CLUSTER reorders once but is not maintained.'},
  {q:'What is a covering index?',opts:['An index that includes every column of the table','An index containing ALL columns a query needs, so the query is answered from the index alone without visiting the table (Index Only Scan)','An index that covers multiple tables','A backup copy of an index'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'If SELECT user_id, created_at FROM orders WHERE user_id=? has an index on (user_id, created_at) — or (user_id) INCLUDE (created_at) in Postgres 11+ — the heap is never touched. Look for "Index Only Scan" in EXPLAIN; keep the visibility map fresh with VACUUM for it to work.'},
  {q:'What does EXPLAIN ANALYZE do that EXPLAIN alone does not?',opts:['Formats output as JSON','Actually EXECUTES the query, showing real row counts, timing per node, buffers — versus the planner\'s estimates only','Analyzes table statistics','Explains the query in plain English'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'EXPLAIN shows the plan with estimated costs/rows; EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs it and reports actual time and rows per node. The key debugging signal is estimate-vs-actual divergence (est 100 rows, actual 2M ⇒ stale statistics or correlated columns — run ANALYZE, consider extended statistics). Careful: it executes DML too — wrap in BEGIN/ROLLBACK.'},
  {q:'What is a partial index in PostgreSQL?',opts:['An index that is only half-built','An index over a filtered subset of rows (CREATE INDEX ... WHERE status = \'pending\') — smaller, faster, and perfect for queries that always target that subset','An index missing some columns','A corrupted index'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'If 99% of orders are \'done\' and your hot query polls WHERE status=\'pending\', a partial index on the 1% is tiny, stays cached, and skips maintenance for the other rows. Also enables conditional uniqueness: UNIQUE (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL.'},
  {q:'What is the N+1 query problem?',opts:['Having one extra index','Fetching a list (1 query) then issuing one additional query PER item (N queries) instead of a join or batched IN () — the classic ORM performance killer','A failed transaction retry','An off-by-one error in LIMIT'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'Load 100 posts, then lazily load each post\'s author = 101 round trips. Fix with JOINs, IN (...) batch loading, or ORM eager loading (includes/select_related/prefetch). Detect via query logs or pg_stat_statements showing the same query with huge call counts.'},
  {q:'Difference between UNION and UNION ALL?',opts:['UNION ALL joins more tables','UNION removes duplicate rows (requires a sort/hash — slower); UNION ALL simply concatenates results, keeping duplicates','UNION ALL removes duplicates','No difference'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'UNION\'s implicit DISTINCT forces deduplication work across the combined set. If duplicates are impossible or acceptable — the common case when combining disjoint partitions — UNION ALL is significantly cheaper. Default to UNION ALL unless you specifically need dedup.'},
  {q:'What is a window function?',opts:['A function that runs during maintenance windows','A calculation across a set of rows RELATED to the current row (OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)) — ranks, running totals, moving averages — WITHOUT collapsing rows like GROUP BY does','A GUI feature','A function on time windows only'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY created_at DESC) tags each user\'s latest order while keeping every row. GROUP BY aggregates rows away; window functions annotate them. Essential interview topic: top-N-per-group, deduplication, running sums, LAG/LEAD comparisons.'},
  {q:'What is a CTE and when does PostgreSQL inline it?',opts:['A Cached Table Expression, always materialized','A Common Table Expression (WITH ... AS) — a named subquery; since PostgreSQL 12, non-recursive CTEs referenced once are inlined into the main query (no optimization fence) unless you write AS MATERIALIZED','A trigger type','An index hint'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'Pre-12, every CTE was an optimization fence (materialized), often catastrophically — filters could not push down. Now the planner inlines simple CTEs like subqueries; use AS MATERIALIZED deliberately when you WANT one evaluation (expensive expression reused), and WITH RECURSIVE for hierarchies.'},
  {q:'How do you efficiently paginate a large table?',opts:['OFFSET 1000000 LIMIT 20','Keyset (cursor) pagination: WHERE (created_at, id) < (?, ?) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 20 — constant-time regardless of depth','SELECT all rows and slice in the application','Random sampling'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'OFFSET n scans and discards n rows — page 50,000 reads 50,000×page_size rows and gets slower linearly (plus rows shift between requests). Keyset pagination seeks directly via an index on the sort key. Trade-off: no "jump to page 500", which most UIs do not need anyway.'},
  {q:'What is table partitioning and its main query benefit?',opts:['Splitting a database across servers','Dividing one logical table into physical child tables by range/list/hash (e.g., monthly by created_at); queries filtering on the partition key scan only relevant partitions (partition pruning), and old data drops instantly via DROP PARTITION','RAID striping','Making backup files smaller only'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'A 2 TB events table partitioned monthly turns "last 7 days" queries into a scan of one partition, and retention becomes O(1) metadata (dropping a partition vs DELETE + VACUUM of millions of rows). Rules: partition key should appear in most queries; keep partition counts sane; indexes are per-partition.'},
  {q:'What is a foreign key ON DELETE CASCADE and its operational risk?',opts:['It deletes foreign databases','Deleting a parent row automatically deletes referencing child rows — convenient, but one small DELETE can silently fan out to millions of rows across cascading chains, causing lock storms and replication lag','It is purely cosmetic','It prevents all deletes'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'CASCADE chains (org → projects → tasks → comments) turn "delete one org" into an unbounded transaction. Many teams prefer ON DELETE RESTRICT + explicit batched cleanup jobs, or soft deletes. Also: index your FK columns — unindexed FKs make parent deletes scan entire child tables.'},
  {q:'Why can SELECT COUNT(*) be slow on a huge PostgreSQL table?',opts:['COUNT is O(n²)','MVCC means visibility is per-row, so an exact count must scan the table (or an index with visibility checks) — there is no maintained global row counter','The * wildcard fetches all columns','Missing GROUP BY'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'Each transaction may see different row sets, so Postgres cannot keep one authoritative counter. For estimates use pg_class.reltuples or EXPLAIN\'s row estimate (instant); for exact counts on subsets, a good index enables an index-only scan; for dashboards, maintain counters via triggers or periodic rollups.'},
  {q:'What does the GIN index type in PostgreSQL excel at?',opts:['Range queries on timestamps','Composite values where one row maps to many index keys: JSONB containment (@>), arrays, and full-text search tsvectors','Sequential integers','Geospatial nearest-neighbor'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'GIN inverts the relationship: each element/key/lexeme points to all rows containing it — ideal for "rows whose array contains X" or "JSONB contains {a:1}". Costs: slower writes and larger size than B-tree (fastupdate pending lists help). GiST handles ranges/geometry/kNN; BRIN handles huge naturally-ordered tables.'},
  {q:'What is the correct type for storing money in PostgreSQL and why not FLOAT?',opts:['FLOAT8 for speed','NUMERIC(precision, scale) — exact decimal arithmetic; FLOAT is binary floating point and cannot represent 0.1 exactly, accumulating rounding errors in sums','TEXT with formatting','MONEY solves everything'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004 in binary floats — unacceptable in ledgers. NUMERIC is exact (arbitrary precision). Alternative pattern: store integer minor units (cents as BIGINT). The MONEY type ties to locale settings and is generally avoided. Same logic applies in every database and language.'},
  {q:'What are generated columns?',opts:['Columns created by AI','Columns computed from other columns (GENERATED ALWAYS AS (expr) STORED) — kept consistent by the database and indexable, e.g., extracting a JSONB field or lower(email) for indexed lookups','Auto-increment IDs only','Temporary query columns'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'STORED generated columns materialize the expression at write time: GENERATED ALWAYS AS ((payload->>\'user_id\')::bigint) STORED gives you a plain indexed bigint over JSONB data. Also the foundation of tsvector columns for full-text search. (Postgres supports STORED; MySQL supports VIRTUAL too.)'},
  {q:'What does REINDEX / index bloat mean in PostgreSQL?',opts:['Adding a second index on the same column','B-tree pages accumulate dead space from MVCC churn; heavily updated indexes grow far beyond their optimal size, wasting cache and I/O — REINDEX CONCURRENTLY rebuilds them online','Rebuilding statistics','A failed CREATE INDEX'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'An index 5x its rebuilt size means most of every page read is dead weight. Detect with pgstattuple / bloat queries; fix with REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY (12+) which builds a fresh copy without blocking writes. Chronic bloat signals hot-update patterns worth revisiting (fillfactor, fewer indexed-column updates).'},
  {q:'Difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE, and DROP?',opts:['They are synonyms','DELETE removes rows (row-by-row, WHERE-able, fires triggers, MVCC dead tuples); TRUNCATE instantly empties the whole table (minimal logging, resets, no per-row triggers); DROP removes the table itself','TRUNCATE is row-by-row','DELETE removes the schema'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'Deleting 100M rows via DELETE writes 100M dead tuples and WAL, then needs VACUUM; TRUNCATE swaps in empty storage in milliseconds (but is all-or-nothing and takes a strong lock). All three are transactional in PostgreSQL — even TRUNCATE and DROP roll back.'},
  {q:'What is a materialized view?',opts:['A view with a nice UI','A view whose result set is physically STORED (and indexable), refreshed on demand (REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW [CONCURRENTLY]) — trading staleness for fast reads of expensive aggregations','A view on materialized columns','An automatically always-fresh cache'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'Plain views re-run their query each time; matviews precompute it — ideal for dashboards over heavy joins/aggregations. Vanilla Postgres refreshes are full recomputations (CONCURRENTLY avoids locking readers but needs a unique index). TimescaleDB continuous aggregates and ClickHouse materialized views refresh incrementally.'},
  {q:'Why is adding a column with a volatile DEFAULT historically dangerous on a big table?',opts:['Columns cannot have defaults','It rewrites the entire table under an exclusive lock; since PostgreSQL 11, a CONSTANT default is metadata-only (instant), but volatile defaults (now(), random(), uuid functions) still force a full rewrite','Defaults corrupt indexes','It disables replication'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN x int DEFAULT 0 is instant on PG11+; DEFAULT now() is not (each row needs its own value). Safe migration pattern: add nullable column → backfill in batches → set default → add NOT NULL (as NOT VALID constraint, then VALIDATE). Zero-downtime migrations are a Staff-level interview staple.'},
  {q:'What is an UPSERT in PostgreSQL?',opts:['An update that runs faster','INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET ... — atomically insert-or-update in one statement, eliminating check-then-insert race conditions','UPDATE followed by INSERT in the app','A replication mode'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'App-level "SELECT then INSERT or UPDATE" races under concurrency (duplicate key errors). ON CONFLICT resolves at the index level atomically; DO NOTHING handles idempotent ingestion. MySQL\'s equivalent is INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE; standard SQL has MERGE (Postgres 15+).'},
  {q:'What is a sequence/identity gap and why is it normal?',opts:['A bug requiring repair','Sequences hand out values non-transactionally — rollbacks, crashes, and caching consume numbers without inserting rows, so IDs have gaps by design; never rely on IDs being consecutive','Evidence of data loss','Caused only by replication'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'nextval() is never rolled back (doing so would serialize all inserts). Gap-free numbering (invoice numbers) requires a dedicated counter table with locking — a deliberate throughput trade. Interview red flag: designs assuming max(id)+1 or unbroken sequences.'},
  {q:'What does statement_timeout protect against?',opts:['Slow typing','Runaway queries — it cancels any statement exceeding the limit, preventing one bad query (missing WHERE, cross join) from consuming resources indefinitely; set sanely per role/application','Network timeouts','Login brute force'],ans:1,cat:'sql',exp:'SET statement_timeout = \'30s\' at the role or connection level is cheap insurance: analyst typos and ORM-generated monsters die before starving the primary. Pair with idle_in_transaction_session_timeout (kills forgotten open transactions) and lock_timeout (fail fast on DDL lock waits).'},

  // ===== NoSQL — ADDITIONS (18) =====
  {q:'What is the CAP theorem?',opts:['A pricing model','In a distributed system experiencing a network Partition, you must choose between Consistency (every read sees the latest write) and Availability (every request gets a response) — you cannot have both during the partition','Cache-API-Persistence layers','A Kubernetes concept'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'CAP is about behavior DURING partitions: CP systems (etcd, ZooKeeper, Spanner) reject requests rather than serve stale data; AP systems (Cassandra, Dynamo-style) keep serving and reconcile later. PACELC extends it: Else (no partition), trade Latency vs Consistency.'},
  {q:'What is eventual consistency?',opts:['Consistency that never arrives','After writes stop, all replicas CONVERGE to the same value given time — reads meanwhile may return stale data; the availability-friendly model of Dynamo-style stores','A synonym for ACID','Consistency checked at month-end'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Cassandra/DynamoDB replicate asynchronously between nodes/regions; a read may hit a replica that has not seen your write. Mitigations: quorum reads/writes (R+W>N), read-your-writes sessions, or strongly consistent read options (DynamoDB ConsistentRead, single-region).'},
  {q:'In Cassandra, what does the partition key determine?',opts:['The column order','WHICH NODE(S) store the row — the key is hashed onto the token ring; all rows sharing a partition key live together and can be read in one efficient query','The replication factor','The compaction strategy'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'PRIMARY KEY ((sensor_id), reading_time): sensor_id (partition key) routes to nodes; reading_time (clustering key) sorts rows within the partition. Golden rules: query by partition key, keep partitions under ~100MB, and avoid hot partitions (e.g., date-only keys where "today" takes all writes).'},
  {q:'What is Cassandra\'s tunable consistency (ONE/QUORUM/ALL)?',opts:['Isolation levels','Per-QUERY choice of how many replicas must acknowledge: ONE (fast, weakest), QUORUM (majority — with W+R>RF gives strong consistency), ALL (strongest, fragile). You dial CAP per operation','A licensing tier','Compression levels'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'RF=3 with QUORUM writes (2) + QUORUM reads (2) guarantees overlap — reads see the latest write, while tolerating one node down. LOCAL_QUORUM confines the quorum to one datacenter for multi-DC latency. This per-request dial is Cassandra\'s signature design.'},
  {q:'What is DynamoDB single-table design?',opts:['Using DynamoDB for a single application','Modeling ALL entities in one table using generic PK/SK attributes and overloaded indexes, pre-shaping data around access patterns so every query is a key lookup — the inverse of relational normalization','A cost-saving billing mode','A migration technique'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'PK=USER#123, SK=ORDER#2026-07-01 puts a user and their orders in one partition, fetched in one Query. You enumerate access patterns FIRST, then design keys/GSIs to serve each. Trade: brutal ad-hoc query flexibility — which is why analytics data gets exported to a warehouse.'},
  {q:'What is a GSI in DynamoDB?',opts:['Global Server Instance','Global Secondary Index — an alternative PK/SK projection of the table, eventually consistent, with its OWN provisioned capacity, enabling additional access patterns','A backup format','A security policy'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'GSIs let you query by attributes other than the table key (e.g., by email, by status+date). They replicate asynchronously (eventually consistent reads only), can throttle the base table if under-provisioned, and cost writes for every indexed-attribute change. LSIs share the partition key and are strongly consistent but rigid.'},
  {q:'How does MongoDB achieve horizontal scaling?',opts:['Bigger servers only','Sharding: collections are split into chunks by a shard key, distributed across shards; mongos routers direct queries, and the config servers track chunk placement','Read-only replicas','Manual table splitting'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'The shard key decision is near-permanent and critical: hashed keys spread writes but lose range queries; ranged keys enable targeted queries but risk hot shards (monotonic keys like ObjectId send all inserts to one shard). Queries without the shard key scatter-gather to ALL shards.'},
  {q:'What is a MongoDB replica set election?',opts:['Choosing a database name','When the primary becomes unreachable, remaining members vote (Raft-like) and a secondary with the most recent oplog becomes the new primary — automatic failover in ~seconds','A sharding operation','User voting on features'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'A replica set (typically 3 members) has one primary taking writes; secondaries tail its oplog. Elections need a majority — hence odd member counts or an arbiter (data-less voter). Writes with w:majority survive failover; w:1 writes on the old primary may be rolled back.'},
  {q:'When are MongoDB embedded documents preferable to references?',opts:['Always','When related data is read together, is bounded in size, and belongs to the parent (order + its line items) — one read, atomic single-document updates; use references for unbounded or many-to-many relations','Never — always normalize','Only for images'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Document modeling rule: "data that is accessed together is stored together." Embedding wins for 1-to-few contained data. Reference when arrays grow unbounded (16MB doc limit; ever-growing arrays are an anti-pattern), when children are queried independently, or for many-to-many.'},
  {q:'What is an LSM tree and which databases use it?',opts:['A balanced binary tree in RAM','Log-Structured Merge tree: writes go to a memtable + WAL, flush to sorted immutable files (SSTables), background compaction merges them — write-optimized storage behind Cassandra, RocksDB, ScyllaDB, LevelDB, and ClickHouse\'s MergeTree family','A network topology','An index only for logs'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'LSM turns random writes into sequential ones (fast ingest); reads may check multiple SSTables (mitigated by bloom filters) and compaction consumes background I/O (write amplification). B-trees (Postgres/InnoDB) are the read-optimized counterpart — THE storage-engine trade-off question in interviews.'},
  {q:'What is a bloom filter\'s role in LSM-based databases?',opts:['Filtering profanity','A tiny probabilistic structure that answers "is key X definitely NOT in this SSTable?" — letting reads skip most files entirely; false positives possible, false negatives impossible','Compressing values','Encrypting keys'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Without bloom filters, a point read might check every SSTable on disk. With them (a few bits per key in RAM), the engine skips files that certainly lack the key, cutting read amplification dramatically. Used by Cassandra, RocksDB, ScyllaDB, HBase, and ClickHouse.'},
  {q:'Why does ScyllaDB outperform Cassandra on identical hardware?',opts:['It skips replication','C++ shard-per-core architecture (Seastar): one shard per CPU core with its own memory and I/O queues — no JVM, no GC pauses, no cross-core locks; typically ~3-10x throughput with flat P99 latency','It stores less data','It uses more replicas'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Cassandra\'s JVM GC pauses create latency spikes and cap per-node throughput; Scylla\'s thread-per-core design with userspace I/O scheduling saturates modern NVMe/network. Same CQL, same data model — teams typically shrink clusters ~3x when migrating. Its Alternator API also speaks DynamoDB.'},
  {q:'What is Redis persistence: RDB vs AOF?',opts:['Two license types','RDB: periodic binary snapshots (compact, fast restart, lose data since last snapshot); AOF: append-only log of write commands (fsync configurable, up to ~1s loss with everysec) — production often enables both','RAM Disk Backup vs Active Online Failover','Deprecated features'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Pure-cache Redis can disable persistence; anything you cannot recompute needs AOF (appendfsync everysec is the standard balance). RDB snapshots complement AOF for fast restarts and backups. Remember: persistence ≠ HA — Sentinel/Cluster replication handles failover.'},
  {q:'What is a Redis sorted set (ZSET) and its killer use case?',opts:['An alphabetically sorted list','Members each with a float score, kept ordered — O(log N) inserts and range queries by rank or score: the canonical LEADERBOARD structure (also rate limiters and priority queues)','A set that expires sooner','A compressed hash'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'ZADD leaderboard 4200 "player:9" ; ZREVRANGE leaderboard 0 9 WITHSCORES = instant top-10 among millions. Skip-list implementation gives logarithmic operations. Also powers sliding-window rate limiting (scores = timestamps) and delayed-job queues (scores = execute-at).'},
  {q:'What is Elasticsearch\'s inverted index?',opts:['An index sorted Z→A','A term → document-list mapping built at index time from analyzed text — finding documents containing "kubernetes" is a direct lookup, not a scan; the core structure of full-text search','A reversed B-tree','An index of deleted docs'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Analysis (tokenize, lowercase, stem) turns text into terms; each term stores its posting list of matching docs with positions/frequencies for relevance scoring (BM25). This is why LIKE \'%word%\' in SQL scans while Elasticsearch answers in milliseconds — completely different data structures.'},
  {q:'Why must you avoid unbounded high-cardinality fields as Elasticsearch mapping keys (mapping explosion)?',opts:['They look ugly','Each new field name adds to the cluster mapping/state; dynamically indexing user-generated field names (e.g., one field per user ID) balloons metadata, destabilizing the cluster — use flattened types or key-value nested docs','Fields cost per-name licensing','It breaks TLS'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Dynamic mapping happily creates fields forever; thousands of fields = giant cluster state synced across nodes, slow updates, OOM risk. Defenses: strict/dynamic:false mappings, the flattened field type, index templates, and modeling variable attributes as {key, value} nested pairs.'},
  {q:'What is a graph database traversal advantage over SQL JOINs for connected data?',opts:['Graphs use less disk','Index-free adjacency: each node stores direct pointers to neighbors, so friend-of-friend depth-4 queries cost proportional to the subgraph touched — while SQL must repeat index-lookup JOINs whose cost grows with table size and depth','SQL cannot express joins','Graphs skip consistency'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'MATCH (me)-[:FRIEND*1..4]->(p) walks pointers; the equivalent 4-way self-JOIN re-probes indexes at every hop. For deep/variable-length relationship queries (fraud rings, recommendations, dependency graphs), native graphs win by orders of magnitude — for flat lookups, they offer no advantage.'},
  {q:'What is etcd\'s core guarantee and why does Kubernetes depend on it?',opts:['Fastest possible writes','Linearizable, strongly consistent key-value storage via Raft consensus — every read can reflect the latest committed write, so the entire cluster agrees on desired state; K8s stores ALL cluster state (objects, leases, config) in it','Unlimited storage','Built-in SQL'],ans:1,cat:'nosql',exp:'Controllers act on etcd watches; split-brain state would mean two controllers fighting. Raft requires a quorum (majority of 3 or 5 members) for writes — lose quorum and the control plane goes read-only. This is CAP\'s CP corner deliberately chosen; etcd performance/health IS Kubernetes health.'},
  {q:'Your p99 latency is terrible but p50 is fine. What is the most likely database-side cause?',opts:['The CPU is slow','Queueing/contention effects — lock waits, connection pool saturation, checkpoint or vacuum storms, or a few queries doing sequential scans while most hit cache','p99 is a meaningless metric','Network cable quality'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'A healthy p50 means the common path is cached and indexed. The tail is where you find lock waits, pool queueing (all connections busy), I/O stalls during checkpoints, and the minority of queries missing an index. Fix tails by looking at wait events (pg_stat_activity.wait_event), not average CPU.'},
  {q:'What does a low buffer cache hit ratio in PostgreSQL indicate?',opts:['Corruption','Reads are going to disk rather than shared_buffers/OS page cache — either the working set exceeds RAM, shared_buffers is undersized, or a large scan evicted hot pages','That indexes are disabled','Replication lag'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Check pg_statio_user_tables: heap_blks_hit vs heap_blks_read. Sustained sub-95% on OLTP means your working set does not fit. Options: add RAM, raise shared_buffers (~25% of RAM), add indexes so scans touch fewer pages, or partition cold data away.'},
  {q:'Why can adding more indexes make a write-heavy workload slower?',opts:['Indexes lock the table permanently','Every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must maintain every index — more B-trees to update, more WAL written, more page splits and bloat, and in PostgreSQL more HOT-update prevention','Indexes only work on reads','They increase network latency'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Index count is a direct write-amplification multiplier. In PostgreSQL, updating an indexed column blocks HOT updates, forcing new index entries and more vacuum work. Audit with pg_stat_user_indexes: idx_scan = 0 means the index costs you writes and buys nothing.'},
  {q:'What is connection pool saturation and why is it worse than it looks?',opts:['Too much water','All pool connections are checked out, so new requests queue — application latency climbs even though the database CPU looks idle, because the bottleneck is waiting for a connection, not executing','It only affects logging','It is a network driver bug'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'A slow query holds its connection; if 20 slow queries occupy a 20-connection pool, every other request stalls. Symptom: app p99 spikes with idle DB. Fix the slow queries, size the pool to cores (not thousands), and use PgBouncer transaction pooling to multiplex.'},
  {q:'What is write amplification in an LSM-tree store like RocksDB/Cassandra?',opts:['Writing in bold','A single logical write is physically rewritten multiple times as compaction merges SSTables through levels — actual disk bytes written can be 10-30x the logical data volume','Writes echoed to replicas','Larger fonts in logs'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'LSM trades read amplification for fast sequential writes, but compaction re-writes data repeatedly. Leveled compaction = higher write amp, better reads; size-tiered = lower write amp, worse reads and more space amp. It is why SSD endurance and compaction throttling matter on these systems.'},
  {q:'Why is OFFSET 100000 LIMIT 20 pathologically slow?',opts:['LIMIT is unoptimized','The database must generate and discard all 100,000 preceding rows before returning 20 — cost grows linearly with page depth','OFFSET locks the table','It disables the query planner'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Deep pagination degrades as users page further. Use keyset/seek pagination: WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 20 — constant time via the index, regardless of depth.'},
  {q:'What does high PostgreSQL table bloat mean for performance?',opts:['Nothing at all','Dead tuples left by UPDATE/DELETE occupy pages until vacuumed — the table and its indexes physically grow, so scans read more pages and cache holds less useful data','Bloat compresses data','It speeds up sequential scans'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'MVCC keeps old row versions; autovacuum reclaims space for reuse but does not shrink files. Long-running transactions and idle-in-transaction sessions hold back the xmin horizon, blocking cleanup. Monitor n_dead_tup; repack with pg_repack (online) rather than VACUUM FULL (exclusive lock).'},
  {q:'What is the impact of a missing index on a foreign key column when deleting a parent row?',opts:['None, FKs are free','Every parent DELETE/UPDATE forces a full scan of the child table to check references — a routine delete becomes a table scan under lock','It corrupts the constraint','It only affects INSERTs'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'PostgreSQL indexes the referenced (parent) key automatically but never the referencing (child) column. This is one of the most common real-world performance bugs: DELETE FROM orders takes minutes because order_items.order_id has no index.'},
  {q:'Why does ORDER BY on a non-indexed column hurt at scale?',opts:['Sorting is banned','The database must materialize and sort the result set — small sorts use work_mem in RAM, but larger ones spill to disk as external merge sorts, adding I/O and latency','It always uses the index anyway','ORDER BY only affects display'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Watch for "external merge Disk: NNNkB" in EXPLAIN ANALYZE. Options: an index matching the sort order (allowing an ordered index scan with no sort node), raising work_mem for that session, or reducing the rows sorted by filtering earlier.'},
  {q:'What is the query planner statistics problem when estimates are badly wrong?',opts:['Statistics are decorative','The planner picks strategies from row-count estimates — if it estimates 10 rows and gets 1,000,000, it may choose a nested loop instead of a hash join, turning milliseconds into hours','Wrong stats corrupt data','It only affects EXPLAIN output'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Compare "rows=" estimated vs actual in EXPLAIN ANALYZE; a 100x gap is the smoking gun. Fixes: ANALYZE, raise default_statistics_target on skewed columns, or CREATE STATISTICS for correlated columns (city and postal_code) where independence assumptions break.'},
  {q:'Why is SELECT * harmful on wide tables?',opts:['It is only a style issue','It ships unneeded columns (including large TEXT/JSONB), inflates network and memory use, defeats index-only scans, and silently breaks when the schema changes','It bypasses the WHERE clause','It disables caching'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'A covering index can answer SELECT id, status entirely from the index; adding * forces heap fetches for every row. Plus detoasting large fields you never read. Naming columns is both a performance and a stability decision.'},
  {q:'What is the thundering herd / cache stampede problem?',opts:['A load-testing tool','A hot cache key expires and hundreds of concurrent requests simultaneously miss and hammer the database with the identical expensive query, often taking it down','Too many replicas syncing','A Kubernetes scheduler bug'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Mitigations: probabilistic early expiration, a mutex/single-flight lock so one request recomputes while others wait or serve stale, background refresh-ahead, and jittered TTLs so keys never expire in lockstep.'},
  {q:'Why does an UPDATE on a single hot row limit throughput regardless of hardware?',opts:['Updates are single-threaded globally','Row-level locks serialize writers on that row — a counter row updated by all requests turns a parallel system into a queue at that row','Hardware never matters','Updates skip the WAL'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'The classic global-counter antipattern. Fixes: sharded counters (N rows summed on read), append-only event rows aggregated later, Redis INCR for the hot path, or batching updates. No amount of CPU fixes a serialization point.'},
  {q:'What does high WAL generation cause in PostgreSQL?',opts:['Faster queries','More disk I/O, larger/longer checkpoints, bigger archive and replication streams, and increased replica lag — heavy updates on wide or heavily indexed tables are the usual source','It has no downstream cost','It reduces backup size'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Every change is written to WAL first for durability. Reduce it with fewer indexes, HOT updates (avoid updating indexed columns, tune fillfactor), avoiding full-page writes storms by tuning checkpoint_timeout/max_wal_size, and using wal_compression.'},
  {q:'Why is a nested loop join catastrophic on large unindexed inner relations?',opts:['It never terminates','For each outer row it scans the inner relation — with no index on the join key, cost approaches rows_outer x rows_inner, so it is excellent for a handful of rows and disastrous for millions','Nested loops are always wrong','It only works on views'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Nested loop is the right choice when the outer side yields few rows and the inner has an index (index nested loop). It appears wrongly when estimates are off. Hash join is the answer for large unindexed joins; look for the planner switching after ANALYZE.'},
  {q:'What is the effect of long-running transactions on PostgreSQL performance?',opts:['They warm the cache','They hold back the xmin horizon so autovacuum cannot reclaim dead tuples anywhere — bloat accumulates database-wide, and locks may block DDL and other writers','They speed up replicas','They compress the WAL'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'One forgotten idle-in-transaction session can bloat a whole cluster. Monitor pg_stat_activity for state = idle in transaction and long xact_start; enforce idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and statement_timeout.'},
  {q:'Why can too-large a batch INSERT be slower than moderate batches?',opts:['Batching is always bad','Huge single transactions consume memory/WAL, hold locks longer, risk timeouts, and lose all work on failure — moderate batches (thousands of rows) amortize per-statement overhead without those costs','Databases cap inserts at 10 rows','Batching disables indexes'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Batching massively beats row-at-a-time (each round trip and commit costs a fsync), but the curve flattens. Use multi-row INSERT or COPY, batches of ~1k-10k, and drop/rebuild indexes for truly bulk loads.'},
  {q:'What is false sharing / hot partition in a distributed database?',opts:['Sharing a password','A partition key with skewed access (e.g., a timestamp prefix or tenant with 90% of traffic) sends disproportionate load to one node — the cluster is scaled but one shard is a bottleneck','Two tables with the same name','Copying data twice'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'In DynamoDB/Cassandra, throughput is per-partition. Sequential keys or one giant tenant create hotspots. Fixes: composite/high-cardinality partition keys, write sharding by appending a hash suffix, or isolating whale tenants.'},
  {q:'Why does the database appear slow when the actual bottleneck is fsync?',opts:['fsync is a myth','Every COMMIT must durably flush WAL to disk — on slow or non-battery-backed storage, commit latency dominates and throughput is capped by fsync rate, not CPU or query complexity','fsync only runs at shutdown','fsync is a network call'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Symptom: trivial single-row commits take milliseconds each. Test with pg_test_fsync. Options: faster storage (NVMe, BBU-backed cache), group commit via commit_delay, or synchronous_commit=off if you can tolerate losing the last few hundred milliseconds on crash.'},
  {q:'What is the right first step when a query suddenly gets slow with no code change?',opts:['Restart the database','Compare the current plan against expectations with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) — data growth, stale statistics, or bloat commonly flip the planner to a different strategy at some threshold','Add more RAM','Drop and recreate the table'],ans:1,cat:'perf',exp:'Plans are not stable; they are re-derived from statistics and data volume. A nested loop that was fine at 10k rows becomes fatal at 10M. Look for estimate-vs-actual divergence first, then ANALYZE, then consider indexing or query rewrite.'},
  {q:'Why should a StatefulSet be used for databases in Kubernetes rather than a Deployment?',opts:['StatefulSets are faster','They provide stable network identities (pod-0, pod-1), stable per-pod PersistentVolumeClaims, and ordered startup/termination — all required for replicated stateful systems','Deployments cannot mount volumes','StatefulSets skip scheduling'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'A Deployment treats pods as interchangeable and may attach the wrong volume or start replicas out of order. Databases need pod-2 to always be pod-2 with its own data and DNS name (pod-2.svc.namespace) so replication topology survives restarts.'},
  {q:'What is the role of a headless Service for a database StatefulSet?',opts:['It hides the database','With clusterIP: None, DNS returns individual pod IPs rather than load balancing — so clients and cluster members can address specific pods (primary vs replicas) directly','It disables networking','It provides TLS termination'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Replication requires addressing a specific member, not a random one. The headless Service gives each pod a stable DNS record. Operators layer additional Services (e.g., a -primary Service with a label selector the operator flips on failover) for client routing.'},
  {q:'Why is a database operator preferable to a raw StatefulSet in production?',opts:['Operators are cheaper','It encodes operational knowledge as a controller — automated failover, backups, PITR, minor-version upgrades, connection routing, and reconciliation of desired state — instead of leaving those to humans and scripts','StatefulSets are deprecated','Operators eliminate the need for storage'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'CloudNativePG, Zalando/Crunchy Postgres, Percona, and Vitess operators watch CRDs and act. A StatefulSet will restart a crashed pod but will never promote a replica, fence a split-brain primary, or run a consistent backup for you.'},
  {q:'What does the PersistentVolume reclaim policy Delete risk for a database?',opts:['Nothing, it is safe','Deleting the PVC destroys the underlying volume and its data — an accidental helm uninstall or namespace delete can permanently erase the database','It only deletes logs','It archives data to S3'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Use reclaimPolicy: Retain for database StorageClasses so the PV survives PVC deletion. Combine with external backups: a reclaim policy is not a backup, it is only protection against one accident class.'},
  {q:'Why should database pods set resource requests equal to limits (Guaranteed QoS)?',opts:['To use more CPU','It gives Guaranteed QoS so the pod is last to be evicted under node pressure and avoids CPU throttling surprises — critical for latency-sensitive stateful workloads','Kubernetes requires it','It disables the scheduler'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Burstable pods get evicted first when a node is under memory pressure — for a database primary that means an unplanned failover. Note memory limits are hard: exceeding one is an OOMKill, so size shared_buffers/heap well below the limit.'},
  {q:'What is a PodDisruptionBudget and why does a database cluster need one?',opts:['A cost cap','It limits how many pods a voluntary disruption (node drain, upgrade) may take down at once — preventing a cluster-wide outage or quorum loss during routine maintenance','It budgets CPU','It restarts pods on a schedule'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Without a PDB, a rolling node upgrade can drain two of three etcd or Postgres members simultaneously, losing quorum. minAvailable: 2 on a 3-member cluster forces the drain to wait. It only constrains voluntary disruptions — not node crashes.'},
  {q:'Why can a liveness probe be dangerous on a database pod?',opts:['Probes are always safe','A probe that fails during a legitimately slow operation (recovery, long checkpoint, heavy load) triggers a restart — turning a slowdown into an outage and potentially a repeated crash loop','Probes consume too much CPU','Liveness probes leak credentials'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Prefer a generous liveness probe that only checks the process is not deadlocked, and put the "is it ready to serve" logic in a readiness probe (which removes the pod from a Service without killing it). Add startupProbe for slow recovery on boot.'},
  {q:'What is the storage requirement for a production database on Kubernetes?',opts:['Any NFS share works','Block storage (a CSI driver providing ReadWriteOnce volumes) with low latency and fsync durability — network file shares like NFS often break fsync semantics and locking assumptions','Only in-memory volumes','emptyDir with backups'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Databases assume POSIX semantics and honest fsync. Local NVMe gives the best performance (with replication for durability, since it is node-bound); cloud block storage (EBS/PD) trades some latency for detachability. emptyDir means data dies with the pod.'},
  {q:'How does a Postgres operator typically route clients to the current primary?',opts:['Clients hardcode pod IPs','The operator maintains a Service whose label selector matches only the pod currently labeled as primary — on failover it relabels pods, and the Service endpoint follows automatically','DNS round robin','Manual kubectl edit'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'CloudNativePG exposes -rw, -ro, and -r Services. Failover = relabel + endpoint update, so applications keep one connection string. Clients still need retry logic: existing connections are severed during the switch.'},
  {q:'Why run etcd on dedicated nodes with fast disks?',opts:['For aesthetics','etcd writes every Raft entry to disk with fsync before acknowledging — disk latency directly becomes control-plane latency, and slow disks cause leader elections and cluster instability','etcd needs GPUs','To reduce licensing'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'The etcd docs recommend p99 fsync under 10ms. Noisy neighbors on shared nodes cause missed heartbeats, leader churn, and apiserver timeouts. Symptom in logs: "apply request took too long" and repeated elections.'},
  {q:'What is the risk of running a database with hostPath volumes?',opts:['None, it is fastest','The data is tied to a specific node with no cluster awareness — a reschedule to another node starts an empty database, and there is no automatic protection against data loss','hostPath is encrypted','It slows down writes'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Use a Local Persistent Volume instead, which the scheduler understands and pins pods to the correct node. Even then, node loss means data loss, so replication and off-node backups are mandatory.'},
  {q:'Why is a rolling update of a StatefulSet database not automatically safe?',opts:['It always is safe','Kubernetes only knows the pod is Ready, not whether the cluster is healthy — restarting the primary or a second member before replication catches up can cause outage or data loss','Rolling updates are disabled','StatefulSets never update'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'This is exactly why operators exist: they perform a controlled switchover (promote a caught-up replica first), verify replication lag, and update the old primary last. Use partitioned rolling updates or OnDelete when doing it manually.'},
  {q:'What does a CSI volume snapshot give a database, and what is its limitation?',opts:['A complete backup solution','A fast, storage-level point-in-time copy of the volume — but it is crash-consistent, not necessarily application-consistent, and lives in the same storage system','Nothing useful','A logical SQL dump'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Postgres survives crash-consistent snapshots via WAL replay, but you should still quiesce or use the operator backup hooks. Critically, a snapshot in the same account/region is not disaster recovery — export to object storage with WAL archiving for PITR.'},
  {q:'Why should database credentials in Kubernetes not live in plain Secrets alone?',opts:['Secrets are perfectly secure','By default Secrets are only base64-encoded in etcd and readable by anyone with get secrets RBAC — enable encryption at rest and prefer External Secrets/Vault with rotation','Secrets cannot hold passwords','Secrets expire hourly'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Layer defenses: encryption at rest for etcd, tight RBAC (namespace-scoped, no cluster-wide get secrets), external secret managers with short-lived or rotating credentials, and audit logging on secret access.'},
  {q:'What is anti-affinity for and why does a database cluster need it?',opts:['To keep pods together','It prevents replicas from being scheduled on the same node or zone — otherwise losing one node could take out multiple members and destroy quorum or availability','It blocks network traffic','It reduces image pulls'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution with topologyKey kubernetes.io/hostname (or topology.kubernetes.io/zone) spreads members. Three replicas on one node is the most common accidental single point of failure in self-managed database clusters.'},
  {q:'Why does a database in Kubernetes still need application-level backups?',opts:['It does not','Kubernetes provides no data protection: it restores pods, not data — you still need logical dumps and/or physical base backups plus WAL archiving to object storage for point-in-time recovery','Backups are automatic','Replicas count as backups'],ans:1,cat:'k8s',exp:'Replicas protect against node failure, not against DROP TABLE, bad migrations, or ransomware — they replicate the mistake instantly. Backups must be off-cluster, tested by restore drills, and ideally immutable.'},
  {q:'What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous replication?',opts:['Speed of the network only','Synchronous makes the primary wait for replica acknowledgment before confirming a commit (zero data loss, higher latency); asynchronous confirms immediately (lower latency, risk of losing recent commits on failover)','Async has no replicas','Sync only works locally'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'This is the core RPO-vs-latency trade. Synchronous means every commit pays a network round trip and a stalled replica can stall writes; PostgreSQL synchronous_commit with a quorum of replicas (ANY 1 OF ...) is a common middle ground.'},
  {q:'What is replication lag and why does it break read-your-own-writes?',opts:['A UI delay','Replicas apply changes after the primary commits — a user who writes then immediately reads from a lagging replica sees stale data, appearing as if the write vanished','Lag only affects backups','It causes data corruption'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Fixes: route reads to the primary for a short window after a write (sticky sessions), use causal/read-your-writes tokens (LSN-aware routing), or synchronous replication. Monitor with pg_stat_replication replay_lag and alert on it.'},
  {q:'What is split-brain in a replicated database?',opts:['A slow query','A network partition leaves two nodes each believing they are primary and accepting writes — the datasets diverge and cannot be automatically reconciled','A CPU scheduling bug','Two replicas syncing'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Prevention requires quorum (a majority must agree on the leader) plus fencing/STONITH so a demoted primary cannot keep serving writes. Never build failover on "the primary looks down to me" from a single observer — that is how split-brain happens.'},
  {q:'What is the difference between physical (streaming) and logical replication in PostgreSQL?',opts:['They are identical','Physical ships WAL byte-for-byte producing an exact block-level copy of the whole cluster; logical decodes WAL into row-level change events, allowing selective tables, cross-version, and cross-schema replication','Logical is always faster','Physical only works for one table'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Use physical for HA replicas and PITR (same major version, whole cluster, replica is read-only). Use logical for major-version upgrades with minimal downtime, selective data distribution, and CDC into other systems.'},
  {q:'What is a quorum and why do replicated systems use odd member counts?',opts:['A meeting requirement','A majority (N/2+1) must agree for a write or election to succeed — odd counts maximize fault tolerance per node, since 3 and 4 members both tolerate only one failure','Odd numbers are faster','It is a licensing rule'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'3 members tolerate 1 failure; 5 tolerate 2. A 4th member adds cost and latency without extra tolerance and increases the chance of an even split. This is why etcd, Raft, and Consul clusters are always 3 or 5.'},
  {q:'What is failover vs switchover?',opts:['The same thing','Failover is unplanned promotion after the primary fails (possible data loss with async replication); switchover is a planned, coordinated role change where the old primary is cleanly stopped first (zero data loss)','Switchover loses data','Failover is manual only'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Use switchover for maintenance and upgrades — the operator waits for the replica to catch up fully, demotes the primary, then promotes. Failover is the emergency path where you accept an RPO defined by your replication mode and lag.'},
  {q:'What is multi-primary (multi-master) replication and its core difficulty?',opts:['It is strictly better','Multiple nodes accept writes, so concurrent conflicting writes to the same key must be resolved — via last-write-wins, CRDTs, or application logic, all of which can lose or mangle intent','It requires no network','It is the PostgreSQL default'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Single-primary avoids the problem by construction. Multi-primary suits geo-distributed writes and offline-first apps (CouchDB, Cassandra, CRDT stores) but you must design merge semantics — LWW silently discards one user\'s change.'},
  {q:'What is a replication slot in PostgreSQL, and its hidden danger?',opts:['A hardware port','It guarantees the primary retains WAL until a specific consumer confirms receipt — but an inactive or dead slot makes WAL accumulate until the primary\'s disk fills and the database halts','Slots compress WAL','It limits replica count'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Slots prevent the "requested WAL segment already removed" failure, at the cost of unbounded retention. Monitor pg_replication_slots for inactive slots and set max_slot_wal_keep_size (PG13+) to cap the damage.'},
  {q:'What is a cascading replica setup used for?',opts:['Faster writes','A replica streams from another replica rather than the primary — reducing network fan-out and CPU load on the primary, useful for many replicas or cross-region topologies','It doubles durability','It enables multi-master'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Trade-off: added latency (lag compounds down the chain) and a mid-chain failure orphans downstream replicas. Common for shipping one stream to a remote region, then fanning out locally there.'},
  {q:'Why is a read replica not a backup?',opts:['It is one, actually','Replication faithfully applies destructive changes — a DROP TABLE or bad migration reaches replicas in milliseconds, so replicas protect against hardware failure, not human or application error','Replicas are read-only','Backups are slower'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'You need a time dimension replication does not have: retained base backups plus WAL archives for PITR, or delayed replicas (recovery_min_apply_delay) that lag by hours as a coarse undo window. Immutable off-site backups defend against ransomware too.'},
  {q:'What does RPO measure and how does replication mode determine it?',opts:['Query latency','Recovery Point Objective is the maximum acceptable data loss — synchronous replication drives RPO toward zero, while async replication makes RPO equal to your replication lag at the moment of failure','Number of replicas','Restore duration'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'RTO (Recovery Time Objective) is about how long to restore service; RPO is about how much data you can lose. Async replicas with 5s lag mean an RPO of ~5s. Quantify both before choosing a topology — cost scales steeply as both approach zero.'},
  {q:'What is Change Data Capture (CDC) and how does it relate to replication?',opts:['A backup tool','It taps the database\'s replication stream (WAL/binlog) to emit row-level change events into other systems — the same mechanism as logical replication, aimed at integration rather than a standby','A load balancer','A caching layer'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Debezium reads MySQL binlog or Postgres logical decoding into Kafka. It beats polling: no missed updates, no timestamp columns needed, low overhead, and correct ordering. It underpins the outbox pattern, search index sync, and cache invalidation.'},
  {q:'What does semi-synchronous replication in MySQL guarantee?',opts:['Full serializability','The primary waits for at least one replica to acknowledge receipt of the binlog event (not necessarily to apply it) before returning commit — a middle ground between async loss and full sync latency','Zero replicas needed','Multi-master safety'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Receipt is not application: the replica has the event durably but may not have executed it, so failover still needs catch-up. It also degrades to async after a timeout — meaning your RPO guarantee silently disappears exactly when the network is bad.'},
  {q:'Why does adding read replicas not solve a write bottleneck?',opts:['It does solve it','Every replica must apply the full write stream — replicas scale reads only, while write capacity remains bounded by the single primary, and each replica adds replication overhead','Replicas accept writes','Writes are always cheap'],ans:1,cat:'replication',exp:'Scaling writes requires sharding (splitting data by key across primaries), a distributed SQL engine (CockroachDB, Spanner, TiDB), or reducing the write volume itself. Bolting on replicas past a point actually increases primary load.'},
];
